    <body>
      <sec id="article-133078.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is a potentially vision-threatening complication that may develop following blunt or penetrating ocular injury. Elevated intraocular pressure after trauma can occur immediately or months to years later, making early recognition and long-term surveillance essential to prevent irreversible optic nerve damage. The mechanisms are diverse and include angle recession, hyphema-related trabecular obstruction, inflammation, lens injury, ghost cell glaucoma, and secondary scarring of the outflow pathways. Because patients often present in emergency settings or after sports, occupational, or road traffic injuries, prompt evaluation and structured follow-up are critical components of care.</p>
        <p>This activity provides a comprehensive review of the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and evidence-based management strategies for traumatic glaucoma. Learners explore acute and delayed presentations, imaging modalities such as gonioscopy and anterior segment optical coherence tomography, and both medical and surgical treatment options. Special emphasis is placed on risk stratification and prevention of long-term optic neuropathy. Participation in this activity enhances interprofessional collaboration among ophthalmologists, emergency physicians, primary care clinicians, nurses, and allied healthcare professionals. By improving recognition, timely referral, and coordinated management, the healthcare team can significantly reduce complications and improve visual outcomes in patients affected by traumatic glaucoma.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;various pathophysiologic mechanisms that lead to traumatic glaucoma, including angle recession, hyphema-related obstruction, and ghost cell glaucoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret the epidemiology of traumatic glaucoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based medical and surgical interventions to control intraocular pressure and prevent optic nerve damage.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional teams, including ophthalmologists, optometrists, nurses, and rehabilitation specialists, to optimize patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=133078&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=133078">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is a type of secondary glaucoma occurring because of various mechanisms related to the trauma of the eye. Eye trauma can be divided into 2 categories: closed-globe injuries and open-globe injuries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;All types of ocular trauma can cause an elevation in intraocular pressure (IOP) in the affected eye through various mechanisms. Without appropriate treatment, this can lead to irreversible glaucomatous damage to the optic nerve and potentially permanent loss of visual function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma represents a spectrum of secondary glaucomatous disorders that develop following blunt or penetrating ocular trauma.&#x000a0;This condition is characterized by elevated IOP resulting from structural and functional disruption of the aqueous humor outflow pathways, ultimately leading to optic nerve damage if untreated. Ocular trauma remains a major global public health concern, accounting for millions of emergency visits annually and contributing significantly to preventable visual morbidity. Among its long-term sequelae, traumatic glaucoma is particularly insidious because it may manifest immediately after injury or develop months to decades later, often when the inciting trauma has been forgotten. The delayed nature of presentation underscores the importance of early identification, lifelong surveillance in high-risk individuals, and comprehensive interprofessional management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>The pathogenesis of traumatic glaucoma is multifactorial and depends on the nature, mechanism, and severity of injury. Blunt trauma, the most common cause, results in anteroposterior compression and equatorial expansion of the globe. This sudden deformation can damage multiple anterior segment structures, including the trabecular meshwork, Schlemm's canal, ciliary body, iris, and lens. Angle recession is one of the most well-recognized mechanisms, characterized by a tear between the longitudinal and circular muscle fibers of the ciliary body. Although angle recession itself does not always lead to glaucoma, extensive damage&#x02014;typically involving more than 180&#x000b0; angle&#x02014;significantly increases the lifetime risk of elevated IOP. The resultant trabecular dysfunction may lead to chronic outflow impairment and progressive optic neuropathy years after the initial trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Penetrating injuries, on the other hand, may cause traumatic glaucoma through direct structural disruption, inflammation, intraocular foreign bodies, or scarring of the anterior chamber angle. Surgical repair of globe injuries can further alter aqueous dynamics. Additionally, retained intraocular foreign bodies, particularly those containing iron or copper, may cause toxic reactions (siderosis or chalcosis), leading to progressive trabecular damage and secondary glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>In the acute setting, elevated IOP may arise from transient mechanisms such as hyphema, inflammation, or lens displacement. Hyphema&#x02014;the accumulation of blood in the anterior chamber&#x02014;is a common consequence of blunt trauma and may obstruct the trabecular meshwork directly or through red blood cell (RBC) breakdown products. Rebleeding, which often occurs within the first week after injury, can exacerbate IOP elevation and increase the risk of optic nerve damage. Inflammatory debris, fibrin, and cellular infiltrates may also impair aqueous outflow during the acute phase. Prompt recognition and management of these mechanisms are essential to prevent permanent sequelae.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Lens-related mechanisms constitute another important pathway. Traumatic cataract formation, lens subluxation, or dislocation can alter anterior chamber anatomy and compromise aqueous drainage. Phacolytic and phacoanaphylactic responses may also occur, particularly with capsular disruption, resulting in secondary inflammatory glaucoma. Similarly, ghost cell glaucoma may develop weeks after vitreous hemorrhage, as degenerated erythrocytes migrate into the anterior chamber and obstruct trabecular outflow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The clinical course of traumatic glaucoma is highly variable. Acute elevation of IOP may present with ocular pain, redness, blurred vision, headache, or nausea, particularly in severe cases. However, chronic traumatic glaucoma&#x02014;most commonly angle recession glaucoma&#x02014;may remain asymptomatic until significant optic nerve damage has occurred. This silent progression highlights the importance of thorough initial evaluation and long-term follow-up after ocular injury, even in patients who appear stable at presentation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Epidemiologically, traumatic glaucoma accounts for a meaningful proportion of secondary glaucomas worldwide. The incidence varies based on geographic region, occupational risk factors, and access to protective measures. Young males are disproportionately affected due to higher rates of sports-related injuries, occupational hazards, and interpersonal violence. In pediatric populations, traumatic glaucoma may have unique presentations and requires careful monitoring due to the potential for amblyopia and long-term visual impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Diagnostic evaluation begins with a detailed history of the injury, including mechanism, timing, and associated symptoms. Comprehensive ophthalmic examination is essential and includes measurement of IOP, slit-lamp biomicroscopy, gonioscopy, dilated fundus examination, and optic nerve assessment. Gonioscopy is particularly critical for identifying angle recession, peripheral anterior synechiae, or angle-closure components. Anterior segment imaging modalities such as ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM) and anterior segment optical coherence tomography (OCT) may provide additional structural detail in selected cases. Visual field testing and OCT of the retinal nerve fiber layer are indispensable tools for detecting and monitoring glaucomatous damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Management strategies depend on the underlying mechanism and disease stage. In acute cases, medical therapy aims to reduce IOP and control inflammation while minimizing complications such as rebleeding in hyphema. Topical beta-blockers, alpha-agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are commonly employed, while caution is exercised with prostaglandin analogs in the presence of active inflammation. Corticosteroids play a pivotal role in controlling inflammatory components. Surgical intervention, including anterior chamber washout, trabeculectomy, glaucoma drainage devices, or minimally invasive glaucoma surgery, may be required in refractory cases. Long-term management necessitates ongoing surveillance, as angle recession glaucoma may manifest years after trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Preventive strategies remain critical in reducing the burden of traumatic glaucoma. Public health initiatives that promote eye protection in high-risk occupations and sports, enforce safety regulations, and educate people on the importance of&#x000a0;prompt medical evaluation after ocular injury are essential components of prevention. Early identification of high-risk individuals and structured follow-up protocols significantly improve visual outcomes. The interprofessional approach to traumatic glaucoma is fundamental to optimizing patient care. Emergency physicians often serve as the first point of contact and must recognize warning signs of elevated IOP. Ophthalmologists play a central role in diagnosis, monitoring, and surgical intervention. Primary care clinicians contribute to systemic evaluation and long-term follow-up, whereas nurses and allied healthcare professionals support patient education and medication adherence. Coordination among these healthcare providers enhances timely intervention, reduces complications, and improves overall outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>In summary, traumatic glaucoma is a complex and potentially delayed consequence of ocular injury that requires vigilance, comprehensive evaluation, and long-term monitoring. The condition's multifactorial pathophysiology&#x02014;encompassing angle recession, hyphema, inflammation, lens disruption, and scarring&#x02014;demands individualized management strategies tailored to the mechanism of injury. Early recognition and interprofessional collaboration are critical in preventing irreversible optic neuropathy and preserving vision. As ocular trauma continues to represent a significant cause of visual morbidity worldwide, heightened awareness and structured surveillance remain indispensable in mitigating the long-term impact of traumatic glaucoma. In this review, glaucoma and ocular hypertension, ie, those without glaucomatous optic disc changes,&#x000a0;are collectively known as traumatic glaucoma to not only facilitate readability but also because optic nerve examination and visual field testing,&#x000a0;two of the main modalities to diagnose glaucoma and distinguish it from ocular hypertension, can be challenging to perform in patients with ocular trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma encompasses a heterogeneous group of secondary glaucomas that arise following blunt or penetrating ocular injury. The etiology is multifactorial and depends on the mechanism of trauma, the structures involved, and the time elapsed since injury. Elevated IOP may occur immediately after trauma due to transient obstruction of aqueous outflow or may develop months to years later as a consequence of structural damage and scarring within the anterior chamber angle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Types of Traumatic Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Closed-globe trauma:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early-onset causes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Traumatic hyphema</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Traumatic iritis</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Trabecular meshwork injury</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Late-onset&#x000a0;causes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Steroid response</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Angle recession</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Lens subluxation and dislocation with or without secondary pupil block</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Phacomorphic glaucoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Phacolytic glaucoma and lens particle glaucoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Ghost cell glaucoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Hemolytic glaucoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Hemosiderotic glaucoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Carotid-cavernous sinus fistula</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Open-globe trauma:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Epithelial downgrowth</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibrous downgrowth</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Mechanisms of Traumatic Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Blunt ocular trauma:&#x000a0;</bold>Blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma is the most common cause of traumatic glaucoma. This condition results from sports injuries, motor vehicle accidents, assaults, falls, or occupational incidents. The sudden anteroposterior compression of the globe leads to equatorial expansion, damaging anterior segment structures. Angle recession is the most characteristic lesion, defined as a tear between the circular and longitudinal muscle fibers of the ciliary body. Although angle recession is common after blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma, only a subset of patients develops glaucoma. Extensive angle involvement (typically &#x0003e;180&#x000b0;) increases the lifetime risk of chronic angle recession glaucoma due to progressive trabecular meshwork dysfunction and fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Other mechanisms following blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema causing mechanical obstruction of the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammatory debris and fibrin deposition</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peripheral anterior synechiae formation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens subluxation or dislocation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic cataract with secondary angle compromise</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ghost cell glaucoma from vitreous hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cyclodialysis cleft leading initially to hypotony, followed by secondary IOP elevation after closure&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Penetrating ocular trauma:&#x000a0;</bold>Penetrating injuries disrupt ocular integrity and may directly damage the trabecular meshwork, Schlemm's canal, iris, or lens. Surgical repair may lead to angle scarring and peripheral anterior synechiae, predisposing to chronic secondary glaucoma.</p>
        <p>Intraocular foreign bodies can induce:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic inflammation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanical angle damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Siderosis (iron toxicity)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chalcosis (copper toxicity)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These toxic effects can progressively impair aqueous outflow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyphema-associated mechanisms:&#x000a0;</bold>Traumatic hyphema is a major cause of early IOP elevation. Elevated pressure may result from:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>RBC blockage of the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Clot obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammatory cell accumulation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rebleeding, which increases the risk of optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Delayed complications include peripheral anterior synechiae and angle scarring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Inflammatory mechanisms:&#x000a0;</bold>Posttraumatic uveitis may impair aqueous outflow through inflammatory cell infiltration and fibrin deposition. Chronic inflammation may cause synechial angle closure and secondary open-angle dysfunction.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Lens-induced mechanisms:&#x000a0;</bold>Trauma can damage the lens capsule, leading to:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacolytic glaucoma (protein leakage)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacoanaphylactic glaucoma (immune-mediated reaction)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pupillary block from lens subluxation or dislocation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These mechanisms may occur acutely or months after injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ghost cell glaucoma:&#x000a0;</bold>This condition occurs weeks after a vitreous hemorrhage. Degenerated erythrocytes (ghost cells) migrate into the anterior chamber and obstruct the trabecular meshwork, causing secondary IOP elevation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Angle closure mechanisms:&#x000a0;</bold>Trauma may induce secondary angle closure through:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammatory membrane formation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior displacement of the lens-iris diaphragm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Choroidal effusion&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r19">[19]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Chemical and thermal injuries:&#x000a0;</bold>Chemical burns may cause limbal stem cell damage and cicatricial changes leading to angle distortion and scarring. Severe thermal injuries may produce similar sequelae.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical trauma:&#x000a0;</bold>Glaucoma may develop after ocular surgeries performed for trauma repair, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lensectomy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vitrectomy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intraocular lens implantation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Repair of cyclodialysis clefts</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Scar formation and altered aqueous dynamics contribute to late-onset IOP elevation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Delayed fibrotic remodeling:&#x000a0;</bold>Even when acute IOP is normal, progressive fibrosis of the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm's canal may lead to late-onset chronic glaucoma years after trauma. This delayed manifestation is a hallmark of angle recession glaucoma.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
