<p>Degenerated RBC obstruction</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 142.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Weeks after VH</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Khaki-colored cells in AC</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 163px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 187px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Synechial angle closure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 142.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Weeks&#x000a0;to months</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Gonioscopic synechiae</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 163px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOFB toxicity</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 187px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Siderosis/chalcosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 142.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Iris heterochromia, retinal changes</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 163px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cyclodialysis cleft closure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 187px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Sudden IOP rebound</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 142.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>After the hypotony phase</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prior low IOP history</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 163px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Post-surgical scarring</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 187px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle fibrosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 142.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>History of globe repair</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 163px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chemical injury</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 187px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cicatricial angle distortion</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 142.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Corneal scarring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations:&#x000a0;AC, anterior chamber; IOP, intraocular pressure; IOFB, intraocular foreign body; RBC, red blood cell; VH, vitreous hemorrhage.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Points</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma is the most common cause of traumatic glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession is the most important long-term risk factor.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema is the most common cause of acute IOP elevation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed-onset glaucoma may occur decades after injury.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy is mandatory&#x000a0;in all traumatic cases.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong follow-up is recommended for extensive angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Eye trauma has been estimated to have an annual incidence of nearly 7 per 1000 population in the United States, with orbital fractures being the most common trauma. Elevation of IOP after repair of open-globe injuries is not uncommon, with one case series showing that 23.3% of patients developed raised IOP (defined as &#x02265;22 mm Hg on&#x000a0;more than 1 visit). Approximately 6.2% of patients in the same study went on to develop glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>A 3.4% incidence of glaucoma following blunt globe trauma has been demonstrated at 6 months follow-up post-injury, but at 10 years post-trauma, it is as high as 10%, highlighting the need for regular follow-up in this patient group.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Traumatic glaucoma is a significant cause of secondary glaucoma worldwide, representing a major source of preventable visual morbidity across all age groups. The condition arises after ocular trauma, which itself accounts for an estimated 55 million eye injuries annually and approximately 1.6 million cases of unilateral blindness each year globally. Within this spectrum, the risk of developing glaucoma varies by mechanism of injury, geographic setting, available protective measures, and healthcare access. Because traumatic glaucoma may manifest immediately or long after the inciting injury, reliable population-based estimates are limited, but epidemiologic trends are well documented across major global regions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Global Prevalence and Incidence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Population-based glaucoma surveys traditionally focus on primary open-angle and angle-closure glaucoma; however, secondary glaucomas&#x02014;including traumatic glaucoma&#x02014;consistently account for approximately 10% to 15% of all glaucoma cases in referral settings. In a large US tertiary glaucoma referral practice, traumatic glaucoma constituted 5% to 12% of secondary glaucoma diagnoses, emphasizing its clinical significance. Incidence estimates of traumatic glaucoma following ocular trauma range from 2% to 20%, depending on injury severity, with higher rates reported in penetrating injuries and extensive angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Regional Variations</bold>
</p>
        <p>The epidemiology of traumatic glaucoma varies considerably across geographic regions, reflecting differences in occupational exposure, socioeconomic conditions, access to protective measures, healthcare infrastructure, and patterns of ocular trauma.</p>
        <p>North America and Europe:&#x000a0;In developed countries such as the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, and Western Europe, blunt&#x000a0;ocular trauma from sports and recreational activities is the dominant mechanism. Occupational and motor vehicle accidents also contribute. Ocular trauma accounts for approximately&#x000a0;10% to 15% of referrals to glaucoma clinics, and traumatic glaucomas resulting from angle recession develop years after injury in up to 10% to 16% of cases with documented significant angle damage. Improved access to emergency care and early intervention likely reduces acute complications, underscoring the importance of long-term monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Latin America:&#x000a0;In Latin American countries, ocular trauma remains prevalent due to high rates of interpersonal violence, road traffic injuries, and industrial accidents. Hospital-based studies from Brazil and Mexico report that trauma accounts for 15% to 25% of all secondary glaucomas, with traumatic glaucoma often presenting at younger ages compared to primary glaucomas. Common sequelae in this region include angle recession and hyphema-associated glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Middle East and North Africa:&#x000a0;Reports from the Middle East, including Saudi Arabia, Iran, and Egypt, indicate ocular trauma rates among the highest globally due to occupational hazards, agricultural injuries, and cultural practices involving fireworks. Traumatic glaucoma in this region represents approximately&#x000a0;10% to 20% of secondary glaucoma diagnoses in tertiary centers. Penetrating injuries, particularly from sharp objects and fireworks, contribute to a higher incidence of angle scarring and long-term glaucomatous change.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Sub-Saharan Africa:&#x000a0;In Sub-Saharan Africa, ocular trauma predominantly occurs in rural and peri-urban settings related to farming, manual labor, and informal occupations with limited protective equipment. Traumatic glaucoma is frequently underdiagnosed due to limited access to ophthalmic services and gonioscopy. Available data suggest secondary glaucomas from trauma contribute to 8% to 18% of the glaucoma burden in referral centers, with delayed presentation common.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>South Asia:&#x000a0;South Asian countries, including India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, and Nepal, report high rates of ocular trauma due to road traffic accidents, industrial work, and agricultural injuries. In large Indian glaucoma clinics, traumatic glaucoma accounts for 10% to 15% of all glaucoma cases, with angle recession and post-hemorrhage glaucoma being the most frequent subtypes. Pediatric trauma and subsequent glaucoma are more common due to a lack of protective eyewear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>East Asia:&#x000a0;In East Asian populations, including China, Japan, South Korea, and Singapore, ocular trauma patterns reflect both industrial and sports-related injuries. Traumatic glaucoma accounts for 6% to 12% of glaucoma referrals. Notably, angle recession glaucoma typically has a delayed onset, often occurring years after the initial injury, underscoring the need for long-term surveillance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Age and Sex Distribution</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma shows distinct demographic patterns.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Age: Younger individuals (15-45 years) are disproportionately affected due to greater exposure to risk-taking activities, manual labor, and sports. However, older patients with systemic comorbidities may develop more severe outcomes due to reduced tissue resilience.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sex:&#x000a0;Globally, males have a higher incidence of traumatic glaucoma, with male-to-female ratios ranging from 2:1 to 5:1 in most cohorts. This disparity reflects greater male participation in high-risk occupations and contact sports.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Specific Subtypes and Their Epidemiology</bold>
</p>
        <p>Angle recession glaucoma:&#x000a0;Angle recession is the most common long-term cause of traumatic glaucoma. Studies show that up to 50% to 60% of eyes with significant angle recession develop elevated IOP within 10&#x000a0;to 15 years post-trauma, although many cases are asymptomatic until optic nerve damage has occurred. The likelihood of glaucoma increases with the extent of angle damage (&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Hyphema-associated glaucoma:&#x000a0;Hyphema following blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma occurs in approximately 30% to 50% of severe injuries. Elevated IOP occurs acutely in 5% to 40% of hyphema cases, depending on clot size, rebleeding, and underlying trabecular dysfunction. Rebleeding, a feared complication, occurs in 3% to 35% of hyphema patients and is strongly associated with secondary glaucomatous damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Ghost cell glaucoma:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Ghost cell glaucoma is a less common, yet well-recognized, delayed secondary glaucoma occurring weeks after vitreous hemorrhage. The precise incidence is difficult to quantify but has been reported in approximately&#x000a0;2% to 15% of eyes with significant vitreous hemorrhage, depending on age and underlying ocular conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Lens-related traumatic glaucomas:&#x000a0;Traumatic cataract and lens subluxation&#x000a0;or glaucoma occur with variable incidence depending on injury mechanism; lens-iris diaphragm disruption is reported in 10% to 20% of severe blunt ocular&#x000a0;traumas, leading to pupillary block or inflammatory glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Risk Factors Influencing Epidemiology</bold>
</p>
        <p>Several factors influence the risk of developing traumatic glaucoma.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severity of trauma: More severe injuries (penetrating, high-impact blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma) have a higher glaucoma risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Extent of angle recession: Greater circumferential involvement correlates with increased risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Age and comorbidities: Older age and preexisting ocular conditions increase vulnerability.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Access to care: Regions with limited access to ophthalmic specialists have higher rates of undiagnosed and advanced traumatic glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occupational exposure: Industrial and agricultural workers have a higher incidence due to hazardous working conditions.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is a globally relevant secondary glaucoma with significant regional and demographic variation. This condition&#x000a0;contributes meaningfully to the global glaucoma burden, particularly in areas with high rates of ocular trauma.&#x000a0;The most common etiologies include blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma, angle recession, and hyphema, with delayed manifestations that necessitate lifelong surveillance. Young males remain the most affected demographic. Regional disparities reflect differences in occupational risks, safety practices, and access to ophthalmic care. Understanding these epidemiologic trends is critical in shaping preventive strategies, surveillance protocols, and public health interventions aimed at reducing vision loss from traumatic glaucoma worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
