      <sec id="article-133078.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Closed-Globe Trauma</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Early-onset causes:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic hyphema
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>As a result of sudden compression-decompression forces onto the globe, the highly vascularized ciliary body and iris, most commonly at the angle, can be torn due to the sudden increase in IOP, limbal tissue stretching, posterior and peripheral flow of aqueous, and posterior displacement of the iris and lens, causing bleeding within the anterior chamber.&#x000a0;Depending on the extent of the blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma, injury can occur to various angle structures. Commonly, damage to the iris insertion manifests as angle recession, followed by cyclodialysis and, very uncommonly, iridodialysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r34">[34]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>The associated injury to vascular structures leads to hemorrhage into the anterior chamber, causing a blood-filled fluid level called hyphema. If blood fills the entire anterior chamber, this is referred to as a total hyphema, also known colloquially as an 8-ball hyphema.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rebleeds, or secondary hemorrhages, can occur 2 to 5 days after the initial bleed and are estimated to have an incidence of 20% to 25% in traumatic hyphema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;This phenomenon&#x000a0;is attributed to fibrinolysis and clot retraction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Rebleeds are often associated with a higher risk of an increase in IOP and worse visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r37">[37]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>The severity of hyphema can be graded by the amount of blood in the anterior chamber, which correlates with an increased likelihood of IOP elevation. Grade I, II, III, and IV hyphemas correspond to less than one-third, one-third to one-half, more than one-half, and the entire anterior chamber being filled with blood, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;Grade I and II are associated with a 13.5% risk of IOP elevation, whereas Grade III is associated with a 27% risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;Grade IV and rebleeds are associated with a 50% risk. The mechanism of IOP elevation is thought to be secondary to obstruction of the trabecular meshwork by RBCs, fibrin, platelets, and other fibrinolysis products.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r39">[39]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic iritis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The mechanism of posttraumatic iritis (anterior uveitis) remains poorly understood. It has been suggested that tissue damage from trauma triggers an innate immune response characterized by increased vascular permeability and an influx of inflammatory mediators.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r40">[40]</xref> Evidence suggests that traumatic iritis may be akin to inflammatory processes observed elsewhere in organ systems, such as the Koebner phenomenon, in which psoriasis flares are precipitated by a minor skin injury in about 25% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r41">[41]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Inflammatory cells and precipitates within the anterior chamber may block the trabecular meshwork, reducing aqueous outflow and causing a secondary increase in IOP. Reduced outflow can also result from inflammation of the trabecular meshwork itself (so-called trabeculitis) caused by trauma, leading to trabecular meshwork congestion and reduced outflow facility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabecular meshwork injury
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Trauma to the eye can mechanically dislodge pigments into the anterior chamber, which may block aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork, thereby increasing IOP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;An increase in trabecular meshwork pigmentation has been associated with an increased risk of the development of traumatic glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;The mechanism of IOP elevation is similar to conditions such as pigment dispersion syndrome and pseudoexfoliative glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r44">[44]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Late-onset traumatic glaucoma can also occur due to trabecular meshwork injury, or more accurately, the tissue healing process&#x000a0;following such injury. Linear<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>tears in the trabecular meshwork, with extension into the Schlemm's canal on UBM, have been reported after blunt trauma to the globe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r45">[45]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Superficial trauma tends to present as a flap at the scleral spur on the gonioscopy, whereas deeper tears may show shredded iris processes. Subsequent scarring along the angle structures can lead to late-onset IOP elevation due to a gradual reduction in aqueous outflow.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage is most commonly associated with intraocular surgery but can also&#x000a0;occur after blunt or penetrating trauma to the globe. The mechanism of hemorrhage in the suprachoroidal space is thought to be secondary to tearing forces on the long or short posterior ciliary arteries, either due to direct trauma or rapid globe decompression&#x000a0;during intraocular surgery and globe penetration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Traumatic glaucoma can occur following IOP elevation from secondary angle closure, due to the mass effect of the hemorrhage in the posterior segment, which displaces the lens and iris forward and causes angle narrowing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r47">[47]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Late-onset causes:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid response
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Topical corticosteroids are commonly used to treat ocular trauma by reducing intraocular inflammation. Many patients develop an associated IOP elevation, most commonly 3&#x000a0;to 6 weeks following the initiation of topical corticosteroid treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;These patients are known as steroid responders. High responders, which constitute 4% to 6% of the population, develop an IOP of more than 31 mmHg or an increase of more than 15 mmHg from their baseline IOP. Moderate responders, comprising about one-third of the population, experience an IOP of 25 to 31 mm Hg or an increase of 6 to 15 mm Hg from baseline. The remainder of the population is nonresponders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r48">[48]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>The mechanism of steroid-induced IOP elevation is believed to&#x000a0;result from&#x000a0;increased outflow resistance at the trabecular meshwork, secondary to the increased expression of glycosaminoglycans and extracellular matrix proteins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r49">[49]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Angle recession is defined histologically as a rupture between the circular and longitudinal fibers of the ciliary muscles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Gonioscopic examination shows a widened ciliary body band and sometimes a newly formed white hyaline membrane covering the trabecular meshwork. This membrane often extends deep into the recessed angle, reducing aqueous outflow, but often many years after the initial traumatic insult, as the formation of the hyaline membrane is a gradual process.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;However, membrane formation has been shown to occur earlier in eyes that have sustained more than 270&#x000b0; of angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Reduced outflow is also felt to be caused by the loss of tension of the ciliary muscle on the scleral spur due to the tearing of ciliary muscle fibers, leading to the narrowing of Schlemm's canal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Unlike hyaline membrane formation, scarring and fibrosis at the angle can also reduce aqueous outflow and elevate IOP. Angle recession is commonly associated with hyphema, which has been reported in 71% to 100% of eyes with angle recession and is the main cause of the initial IOP spike.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens subluxation and dislocation with or without secondary pupil block
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Ocular trauma can cause posterior displacement of the lens-iris diaphragm, which can subsequently rupture the zonular fibers that hold the lens in place. A lens may be subluxated when greater than 25% of the zonules are torn, such that the lens is partially displaced from its normal centralized position.&#x000a0;Lens dislocation occurs when all or most of the zonules are completely disrupted. The lens can then become dislocated into the anterior chamber through the pupil or into the vitreous cavity. IOP elevation can occur through several mechanisms, including pupil block with intermittent or acute angle-closure by a subluxated lens, the release of pigments from chafing of a subluxated or dislocated lens on the iris, and trabecular meshwork blockage from vitreous strands prolapsed into the anterior chamber, in the setting of a frankly dislocated lens into the vitreous cavity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r52">[52]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacomorphic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Trauma, both blunt and globe-penetrating (without breaking the lens capsule), can lead to the rapid development of a large intumescent cataract. The enlarged lens can lead to secondary angle closure due to forward displacement of the iris and subsequent pupillary block.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r53">[53]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacolytic glaucoma and lens particle glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypermature cataracts that occur after trauma may be associated with microscopic defects of the lens capsule due to the rapid stretching of the capsule at the time of injury. Soluble lens protein can leak through these defects into the anterior segment. As macrophages phagocytose these proteins, they can ultimately block the trabecular meshwork, leading to phacolytic glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;High-molecular-weight lens protein has also been suggested to directly obstruct the trabecular meshwork and impede aqueous outflow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r55">[55]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Although phacolytic and lens particle glaucoma share some similarities, they are distinct entities. Pathophysiologically, lens particle glaucoma is caused by actual lens particles rather than soluble lens protein, which deposits on the trabecular meshwork and obstructs outflow. These lens particles can come from the traumatic rupture of the lens capsule.&#x000a0;These particles can also originate from the cataract itself during cataract surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rarely, Elsching pearls&#x02014;clusters of proliferating lens epithelial cells formed in the equatorial region of the capsular bag&#x02014;may&#x000a0;migrate to the posterior capsule and contribute to posterior capsular opacification after cataract surgery. These pearls can be released into the aqueous by an Nd:YAG capsulotomy laser, subsequently causing obstruction of aqueous outflow.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacoantigenic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Phacoantigenic glaucoma, also known as phacoanaphylactic glaucoma, is a latent, chronic granulomatous reaction to retained lens material after trauma or cataract surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Inflammation can occur in the affected or fellow eye after a latent period of immunological sensitization to crystalline lens antigens, previously enclosed by the lens capsule and hitherto unencountered by the body's immune system. This process is believed to be a type III hypersensitivity reaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r56">[56]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ghost cell glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Ghost cells are old, spherical, degenerated, and depigmented RBCs that also contain clumps of degenerated residual hemoglobin bound to the cell membrane, called Heinz bodies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;These cells can arise after a longstanding vitreous hemorrhage following trauma, as well as in the context of other pathologies such as proliferative diabetic retinopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r58">[58]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Due to trauma or previous surgery, the anterior hyaloid face of the vitreous may be disrupted, allowing ghost cells to pass from the vitreous cavity to the anterior chamber. Because these cells are rigid, they can obstruct the pores of the trabecular meshwork, thereby reducing aqueous outflow.&#x000a0;The formation of ghost cells occurs within 1&#x000a0;to 3 weeks after the initial vitreous hemorrhage, but the rise in IOP often occurs 1&#x000a0;to 3 months after.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r59">[59]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ghost cell glaucoma is uncommon in&#x000a0;cases&#x000a0;of an isolated hyphema unless very longstanding. High oxygen levels and high aqueous circulation rate within the anterior chamber allow clearance of the hyphema before the RBCs can degenerate into ghost cells.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemolytic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hemolytic glaucoma may be confused with ghost cell glaucoma. In hemolytic glaucoma, RBCs from either a vitreous hemorrhage or an anterior chamber hyphema are broken down into hemoglobin and hemosiderin. These products, as well as hemoglobin-filled macrophages, block the aqueous outflow pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r60">[60]</xref>&#x000a0;On examination, cells in the anterior chamber in hemolytic glaucoma have a red tinge, whereas cells in ghost cell glaucoma are khaki-colored.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemosiderotic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hemosiderotic glaucoma is a rare entity caused by the direct toxic effect of intraocular iron on the trabecular meshwork, resulting in fibrosclerosis and subsequent obstruction of aqueous outflow. The source of intraocular iron can be introduced through trauma, such as an intraocular foreign body, or through blood in the anterior chamber. The hemoglobin in RBCs is released after hemolysis and, in turn, is further degraded into hemosiderin, which contains toxic granules of inorganic iron.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r57">[57]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Carotid-cavernous sinus fistula
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This type of fistula can develop after trauma to the orbit. Carotid-cavernous sinus fistula can be subdivided into 2 subtypes: a direct high-flow shunt with communication between the internal carotid artery and cavernous sinus, and an indirect low-flow dural shunt with communication between cavernous arterial branches and the cavernous sinus. Traumatic carotid-cavernous sinus fistula is more frequently high-flow. Other causes of the high-flow fistula may include rupture of an internal carotid artery aneurysm within the cavernous sinus, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome type IV, and iatrogenic causes such as an internal carotid artery endarterectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Low-flow fistula can be caused by hypertension, fibromuscular dysplasia, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome type IV, and dissection of the internal carotid artery. Carotid-cavernous sinus fistula can lead to a raised IOP via increased episcleral venous pressure, which can, in turn, cause angle closure due to edema of the ciliary body and choroid. Raised episcleral venous pressure can also lead to central retinal vein occlusion, leading to ischemia and subsequent iris neovascularization and neovascular glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r22">[22]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Open-Globe Trauma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Please refer to "<bold>Closed-Globe Trauma</bold>" in the <bold>Pathophysiology</bold> section for mechanisms by which&#x000a0;globe-penetrating injury may lead to secondary intraocular pressure elevation and glaucoma. However, the following mechanisms are specific to eye injuries in which the corneoscleral envelope is breached through penetration or rupture.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Epithelial downgrowth:&#x000a0;</bold>Epithelial downgrowth occurs when non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelial cells&#x02014;most commonly originating from the conjunctiva and cornea&#x02014;are introduced through a wound into the anterior chamber. Epithelial downgrowth can present in several forms, including epithelial pearls, cysts, and membranes. Epithelial pearls are small structures on the iris surface, often far from the original entry site of the trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Epithelial cysts are often found near or in continuity with the wound. The first 2 forms tend to be more benign;&#x000a0;however, epithelial membranes proliferate aggressively and thus have the greatest potential to elevate IOP and cause glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r61">[61]</xref></p>
        <p>Elevation of the IOP occurs when epithelial membrane growth over the trabecular meshwork obstructs aqueous outflow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;Peripheral anterior synechiae may form as epithelial cells proliferate, triggering an inflammatory response that can further accelerate their formation. These epithelial cells within the anterior chamber may also contain aberrant goblet cells that secrete mucin, thereby blocking the trabecular meshwork.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r62">[62]</xref>&#x000a0;Membrane formation across the anterior lens and pupil may also lead to pupil block.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Fibrous downgrowth:&#x000a0;</bold>Fibrous downgrowth can appear similar to epithelial downgrowth but&#x000a0;is more common, benign, and indolent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;Fibrous downgrowth often presents as a thick fibrovascular membrane in the anterior chamber or as a retrocorneal membrane covering the corneal endothelium, trabecular meshwork, and iris, leading to IOP elevation due to reduced aqueous outflow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;The origin of fibrous tissue is not well understood, but it may arise from subconjunctival connective tissue, stromal keratocytes, or metaplastic corneal endothelial cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histopathologic changes in traumatic glaucoma reflect both the primary mechanical injury and the secondary reparative and fibrotic processes that evolve over time. The microscopic findings vary depending on the mechanism of trauma (blunt versus penetrating), the interval since injury (acute versus chronic), and the structures involved.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r64">[64]</xref> The most characteristic histologic feature is damage to the trabecular meshwork and ciliary body, particularly in cases of angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r65">[65]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Angle Recession (Most Characteristic Lesion)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Angle recession is the hallmark histopathologic finding following blunt ocular trauma. This lesion results from a tear between the circular and longitudinal fibers of the ciliary body.</p>
        <p>The microscopic findings include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Separation of circular and longitudinal ciliary muscle fibers</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Widening of the ciliary body band</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disruption and loss of trabecular endothelial cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Collagen disorganization within the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Microhaemorrhages in the angle structures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive fibrosis in chronic cases</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Over time, the trabecular meshwork undergoes degenerative remodeling, with:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of trabecular beams</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Thickened and sclerosed beams</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased extracellular matrix deposition</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased cellularity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Obliteration of the Schlemm's canal in severe cases</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These changes impair aqueous outflow and lead to chronic elevation of IOP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyphema-Related Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Following traumatic hyphema, RBCs and inflammatory debris accumulate in the anterior chamber.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Acute phase:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Erythrocytes within the trabecular meshwork spaces</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibrin strands obstructing trabecular beams</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Macrophage infiltration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endothelial cell swelling</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic phase:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemosiderin deposition in trabecular cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibrotic thickening of trabecular beams</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peripheral anterior synechiae formation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Persistent obstruction and scarring result in secondary open-angle glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r66">[66]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Inflammatory (Uveitic) Mechanisms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Trauma frequently induces anterior uveitis.</p>
        <p>The histologic features include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lymphocytic and macrophage infiltration in the iris and ciliary body</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Plasma cells in chronic inflammation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibrin exudation into the anterior chamber</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Posterior synechiae formation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peripheral anterior synechiae leading to angle closure</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Chronic inflammation promotes fibroblast proliferation and extracellular matrix deposition, permanently narrowing the angle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Lens-Induced Traumatic Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic disruption of the lens capsule leads to leakage of lens proteins.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Phacolytic mechanism:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Macrophages filled with lens proteins obstruct the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Proteinaceous material within outflow channels</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Phacoanaphylactic mechanism:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Granulomatous inflammatory response</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multinucleated giant cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dense lymphocytic infiltration</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These mechanisms may contribute to secondary inflammatory glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ghost Cell Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ghost cell glaucoma occurs weeks after a vitreous hemorrhage.</p>
        <p>The microscopic findings include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Degenerated erythrocytes (ghost cells) lacking hemoglobin</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rigid, khaki-colored cells within the anterior chamber</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanical obstruction of the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The ghost cells are less deformable and obstruct aqueous outflow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Peripheral Anterior Synechiae</bold>
</p>
        <p>Chronic trauma-related inflammation may lead to peripheral anterior synechiae formation.</p>
        <p>The histopathologic findings include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Iris stroma adherent to the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibrovascular membrane formation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Permanent angle closure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Secondary synechial scarring&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r67">[67]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Cyclodialysis and Subsequent Fibrosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma may cause a cyclodialysis cleft.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Acute phase:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Separation of the ciliary body from the scleral spur</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypotony</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Healing phase:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibrous tissue proliferation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle distortion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Secondary elevated IOP after cleft closure&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r68">[68]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Retained Intraocular Foreign Body</bold>
</p>
        <p>Metallic intraocular foreign bodies cause toxic degeneration.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Siderosis bulbi:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Iron deposition in the trabecular meshwork</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pigmented trabecular cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Degenerative changes in the ciliary epithelium</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal degeneration</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Chalcosis:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Copper deposition</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greenish discoloration in tissues</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic inflammatory response</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These toxic effects progressively damage aqueous outflow structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r69">[69]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Optic Nerve Head Changes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Chronic IOP elevation results in glaucomatous optic neuropathy.</p>
        <p>The microscopic features include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of retinal ganglion cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Thinning of the retinal nerve fiber layer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cupping of the optic disc</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lamina cribrosa deformation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Axonal loss</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These findings are similar to primary open-angle glaucoma but occur secondary to trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r70">[70]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
