          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: RBC, red blood cell.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Summary of Histopathologic Progression</bold>
</p>
        <p>Acute trauma leads to structural disruption, followed by an inflammatory response and cellular infiltration, which progress to fibrosis and scarring. These changes cause trabecular dysfunction, resulting in chronic IOP elevation and, ultimately, optic nerve damage.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Microscopic Hallmarks</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession (separation of ciliary muscle fibers)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabecular meshwork fibrosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ghost cell obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens protein&#x02013;induced inflammation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic nerve cupping in advanced disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r71">[71]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s7" sec-type="Toxicokinetics">
        <title>Toxicokinetics</title>
        <p>Toxicokinetics in traumatic glaucoma primarily relate to the ocular distribution, metabolism, accumulation, and clearance of toxic substances that result from trauma or therapeutic interventions. Unlike primary glaucomas, traumatic glaucoma may involve exposure to endogenous blood products, lens proteins, metallic ions from intraocular foreign bodies, inflammatory mediators, and exogenous pharmacologic agents. Understanding toxicokinetics is essential for preventing progressive trabecular damage, optic neuropathy, and irreversible vision loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Blood Breakdown Products (Post-Hyphema)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Following blunt trauma with hyphema, erythrocytes enter the anterior chamber and undergo degradation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Absorption and distribution:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>RBCs disperse within aqueous humor.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemoglobin is released and metabolized into hemosiderin and iron-containing compounds.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>These products accumulate within trabecular endothelial cells.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Local toxic effects:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Iron deposition induces oxidative stress.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabecular endothelial cell apoptosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive outflow dysfunction.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Clearance:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Normally cleared via the trabecular meshwork and macrophages.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent hyphema or rebleeding overwhelms clearance mechanisms.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Chronic hemosiderin deposition may lead to secondary open-angle glaucoma due to cumulative trabecular toxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ghost Cell Toxicokinetics</bold>
</p>
        <p>After a vitreous hemorrhage, degenerated erythrocytes (ghost cells) migrate anteriorly.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key characteristics:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dehemoglobinized RBCs become rigid and less deformable.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Poor phagocytic clearance compared to fresh RBCs.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanical obstruction of the trabecular meshwork.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These cells persist longer in the aqueous humor, prolonging the IOP elevation until they are removed surgically or cleared naturally.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Lens Protein Toxicity</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic disruption of the lens capsule releases high-molecular-weight lens proteins into the anterior chamber.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Toxicokinetic profile:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Proteins diffuse into the aqueous humor.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Phagocytosed by macrophages.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Protein-laden macrophages obstruct the trabecular meshwork.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In phacolytic glaucoma, the high protein load exceeds clearance capacity, resulting in inflammatory obstruction and elevated IOP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Metallic Ion Toxicity (Intraocular Foreign Body&#x02013;Related)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intraocular foreign bodies, particularly iron or copper, cause progressive metal ion release.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Siderosis bulbi (iron toxicity):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Absorption
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Iron ions diffuse into the intraocular tissues</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Distribution
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Accumulate in the trabecular meshwork, ciliary epithelium, and retina</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cellular effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Free radical generation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lipid peroxidation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mitochondrial dysfunction</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Outcome
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Progressive trabecular sclerosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Secondary glaucoma</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Chalcosis (copper toxicity):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Copper ions deposit in the Descemet membrane and the trabecular meshwork.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Less destructive than iron but still associated with a chronic inflammatory response.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Metal ion toxicity is dose- and time-dependent; earlier removal reduces cumulative damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r72">[72]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Inflammatory Mediator Kinetics</bold>
</p>
        <p>Trauma triggers the release of several cytokines, including&#x000a0;interleukin-1, tumor necrosis factor-alpha, transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-&#x003b2;), and prostaglandins.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Local effects:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Increase vascular permeability.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Promote fibroblast activation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stimulate extracellular matrix deposition.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>TGF-&#x003b2; particularly contributes to trabecular fibrosis and long-term outflow resistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r73">[73]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pharmacologic Toxicokinetics in Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma treatment itself may introduce toxic considerations.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Topical medications:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Beta blockers: Minimal ocular toxicity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostaglandin analogues: May exacerbate inflammation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: Localized corneal effects.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Corticosteroids:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid responders may develop secondary IOP elevation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Accumulation in the trabecular meshwork alters extracellular matrix remodeling.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical adjuncts:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Mitomycin C: Inhibits fibroblast proliferation but may cause scleral thinning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r74">[74]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Oxidative Stress and Free Radical Damage</bold>
</p>
        <p>Blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma generates reactive oxygen species.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Mechanisms:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanical cell injury.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iron-catalyzed Fenton reaction.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mitochondrial disruption.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Persistent oxidative stress accelerates trabecular degeneration and optic nerve vulnerability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r75">[75]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4. Toxicokinetic Mechanisms in Traumatic Glaucoma&#x000a0;</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
