        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: TM, trabecular meshwork;&#x000a0;AC, anterior chamber; AH, aqueous humor; IOFB, intraocular foreign body; ECM, extracellular matrix; IL-1, interleukin-1; TNF-&#x003b1;, tumor necrosis factor-alpha; TGF-&#x003b2;, transforming growth factor-beta; IOP, intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>Toxicokinetics in traumatic glaucoma involve the intraocular accumulation, persistence, and clearance of blood products, lens proteins, metal ions, inflammatory mediators, and pharmacologic agents. These substances disrupt trabecular meshwork function through oxidative stress, cellular apoptosis, fibrosis, and mechanical obstruction. The cumulative burden of toxic exposure determines the risk of chronic IOP elevation and optic nerve damage. Early intervention&#x02014;particularly removal of intraocular foreign bodies and management of hyphema&#x02014;can significantly reduce long-term toxic sequelae.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s8" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The remainder of this discussion focuses on managing elevated IOP as a sequel to the traumatic event and assumes that the acute injury has been treated; the management of ocular trauma in the acute setting is not discussed further.</p>
        <p>
<bold>History</bold>
</p>
        <p>A detailed history of the trauma should be&#x000a0;obtained and carefully documented. The specific details of the trauma should be noted, including the time, nature, and setting of the event. For example, this may involve an alleged assault or accidental injury at home, workplace, or during leisure activities. Any possibility of foreign body involvement&#x000a0;should also be recorded, including its distance, trajectory, and speed, if relevant.</p>
        <p>Clinicians should record which treatment, and, if applicable, which surgery, has been carried out for the trauma. The timing of IOP elevation relative to the injury and treatment should be determined. Any past ophthalmic and medical histories, such as a preexisting history of glaucoma, previous eye surgery, bleeding disorders, sickle cell disease, the use of topical and systemic steroids, and the use of anticoagulation, should be recorded. A family history of sickle cell disease and blood dyscrasias should also be obtained.</p>
        <p>Depending on the history, the possibility of traumatic optic neuropathy and traumatic brain injury needs to be considered, as this may influence the interpretation of the physical examination and the results of visual field testing. Old correspondence from previous treating clinicians, hospital discharge letters, and imaging reports, including the images themselves, may also require evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r76">[76]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Mechanism of injury:&#x000a0;</bold>The type of trauma significantly influences the risk and mechanism of glaucoma. Clinicians should inquire about:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma (sports injury, assault, and road traffic accident)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Penetrating injury (sharp objects and metallic fragments)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Blast injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chemical or thermal injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occupational exposure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Previous ocular surgery following trauma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The patient may not recall minor trauma, especially in cases of delayed angle recession glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Timing of symptoms:&#x000a0;</bold>The temporal relationship between the injury and symptom onset is crucial.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Immediate elevation of IOP: Suggests hyphema, inflammatory obstruction, or lens displacement.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subacute onset (weeks): Consider ghost cell glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed onset (months to years): Suggests angle recession or progressive trabecular fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Visual symptoms:&#x000a0;</bold>Patients may report:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Blurred or decreased vision</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Halos around lights</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular pain or headache</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Photophobia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intermittent vision fluctuations</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Chronic angle recession glaucoma may be asymptomatic until advanced optic nerve damage occurs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r77">[77]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Associated symptoms:&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>History of hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent eye redness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>History of vitreous hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptoms of lens dislocation (monocular diplopia)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nausea or vomiting in acute IOP elevation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r78">[78]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Past ocular history:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Prior glaucoma diagnosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Previous IOP measurements</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of topical steroids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Previous angle evaluation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Family history of glaucoma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Visual acuity, IOP (ideally measured with Goldmann applanation tonometry), and pupillary reflexes (including assessment for a relative afferent pupillary defect) should be checked. Assessing other features of optic nerve dysfunction, including red and brightness desaturation, and dyschromatopsia with color plate testing, may help determine whether a component of optic nerve dysfunction&#x000a0;is attributed to non-glaucomatous causes, such as traumatic optic neuropathy.</p>
        <p>To make an appropriate adjustment for the IOP measurement, the central corneal thickness should be measured using ultrasound pachymetry, anterior segment OCT, and Schleimpflug (Pentacam) tomography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Depending on how recent the injury occurred, the anterior chamber may show the presence of a hyphema; inflammatory cells or fibrin in cases of traumatic iritis; lens protein particles or lens material in cases of phacolytic, lens particle, and phacoantigenic glaucoma; brownish-colored cells corresponding to RBCs found in hemolytic glaucoma; and&#x000a0;khaki-colored cells corresponding to ghost cells in ghost cell glaucoma.</p>
        <p>The corneal endothelium may show keratic precipitates from uveitis and corneal blood staining from a previous, longstanding hyphema. Iris examination may reveal iridodialysis, transillumination defects due to foreign body entry, and epithelial pearls from downgrowth. Signs of posterior synechiae may be&#x000a0;present at the pupil margin, indicating previous inflammation. The anterior chamber&#x000a0;may be shallow in secondary narrow-angle or angle-closure glaucoma. The anterior chamber<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>may also show signs of epithelial and fibrous downgrowth as an epithelial membrane or a fibrovascular membrane, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Gonioscopy may show signs of trauma to the angle, including hemorrhage, trabecular meshwork hyperpigmentation from trabecular meshwork injury, peripheral anterior synechiae, and angle recession.</p>
        <p>The lens may show signs of subluxation, including iridodonesis, phacodonesis, asymmetric anterior chamber depth, visibility of parts of the lens equator when the pupil is dilated, strands of vitreous at the pupil margin, or frank prolapse of vitreous into the AC; dislocation; enlarged lens thickness; and disrupted capsule in phacolytic and lens particle glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Posterior segment examination may reveal the presence of ghost cells, retained foreign body, vitreous hemorrhage, retinal hemorrhage, commotio retinae, retinal breaks with or without associated rhegmatogenous detachment, suprachoroidal hemorrhage, or choroidal rupture. If glaucoma has already progressed, the optic discs may appear cupped.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>A comprehensive ophthalmic examination should include assessment of visual acuity, IOP measurement, slit-lamp examination, gonioscopy, fundus examination, visual field testing, and appropriate ocular imaging when indicated.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual acuity:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Baseline acuity assessment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Comparison with the fellow eye</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluation for amblyopia in pediatric cases&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r78">[78]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular pressure:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Measured by applanation tonometry</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>May be elevated, normal, or occasionally low (eg, cyclodialysis cleft)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP asymmetry between eyes is suggestive</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Slit-lamp examination (anterior segment findings):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema (layered blood or microhyphema)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior chamber cells and flare</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iris sphincter tears</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iridodialysis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens subluxation or cataract</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal blood staining (severe hyphema)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Gonioscopy (critical examination):&#x000a0;</bold>Gonioscopy is mandatory&#x000a0;in all traumatic cases. Findings may include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession (widened ciliary body band)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabecular pigmentation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cyclodialysis cleft</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle closure components</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Angle recession typically appears as a broad, irregular ciliary body band compared to the fellow eye.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Fundus examination:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic nerve head cupping</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rim thinning</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal nerve fiber layer defects</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vitreous hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Choroidal rupture&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r79">[79]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Visual field testing:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Detects early glaucomatous defects</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Arcuate scotomas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasal step</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Paracentral defects</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Imaging (when indicated):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior segment OCT</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>UBM</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>OCT retinal nerve fiber layer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>B-scan if media opacity is present&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r80">[80]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 5. Key History Clues in Traumatic Glaucoma</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>History Element</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical Implication</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Risk of angle recession</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35.8704px;">
                <td style="height: 35.8704px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Penetrating injury</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.8704px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle scarring and IOFBs</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema history</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute obstruction risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Vitreous hemorrhage</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ghost cell glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed symptoms</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic angle fibrosis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 265.849px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroid use</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 305.151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroid-induced IOP elevation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations:&#x000a0;IOFBs, intraocular foreign bodies; IOP, intraocular pressure.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 6. Physical Examination Findings</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Examination Component</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Findings</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Significance</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Visual acuity</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Reduced</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Optic nerve damage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elevated</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Diagnostic hallmark</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Slit-lamp examination</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute mechanism</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Gonioscopy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Long-term risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Fundus examination</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cupping</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 259px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Visual field testing</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 234.948px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Scotomas</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 263.052px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Functional damage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: IOP,&#x000a0;intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Red Flags Suggesting Traumatic Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP asymmetry &#x0003e;5 mmHg</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Extensive angle recession (&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive optic disc cupping</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>History of intraocular foreign bodies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Unexplained unilateral glaucoma in a young patient</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Paediatric Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Children may present differently, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Poor cooperation during the examination</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Risk of amblyopia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher inflammatory response</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Long lifetime risk of angle recession glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>A detailed history focusing on the mechanism and timing of injury, combined with careful slit-lamp examination and mandatory gonioscopy, is essential in diagnosing traumatic glaucoma. Because delayed presentation is common, long-term follow-up is critical, particularly in patients with extensive angle recession or significant anterior segment injury.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s9" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>An ultrasound B-scan can be useful if the fundal view is blocked, for example, by dense, longstanding vitreous hemorrhage.</p>
        <p>In certain situations, more specific investigations may be necessary. UBM may be useful in traumatic cataracts for detecting and assessing occult zonular defects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;UBM is also useful for evaluating angle structures and the anterior segment, including the ciliary body, in the settings of suspected angle recession, iridodialysis, and cyclodialysis cleft.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r82">[82]</xref> UBM aids diagnosis, especially when the view of the anterior segment is limited by media opacities or anatomical distortion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r82">[82]</xref></p>
        <p>Specular microscopy may be helpful in epithelial downgrowth. This technique may show a demarcation line between the epithelial membrane and the corneal endothelium in the setting of a retrocorneal epithelial membrane. However, its use requires a relatively clear cornea and&#x000a0;may not be useful in the presence of corneal edema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r83">[83]</xref>&#x000a0;Anterior segment OCT may be useful for determining the origin of epithelial downgrowth. However, an aqueous tap and cytology or a tissue biopsy of the membrane are required for a definitive diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Evaluation of traumatic glaucoma requires a systematic, mechanism-based approach because IOP elevation may occur acutely, subacutely, or years after the inciting injury. The goal of evaluation is to identify the mechanism of IOP elevation, assess the extent of structural damage, detect optic nerve injury, determine disease severity, and guide treatment planning.</p>
        <p>A high index of suspicion is essential in any patient with a history of ocular trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Initial Clinical Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Detailed history:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanism of injury (blunt versus penetrating versus chemical)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Timing of injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Immediate versus delayed symptoms</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>History of hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prior ocular surgeries</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid exposure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Family history of glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Even remote trauma (years prior) must be documented, as angle recession glaucoma may present decades later.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Comprehensive Ophthalmic Examination </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual acuity:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Establish baseline acuity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Compare with fellow eye</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Identify amblyopia risk in children&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r77">[77]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular pressure:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Goldmann applanation tonometry (preferred)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP asymmetry &#x0003e;5 mm Hg is suspicious</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low IOP initially may suggest cyclodialysis cleft</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevated IOP may be acute or chronic</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Slit-lamp examination:&#x000a0;</bold>The&#x000a0;following conditions are evaluated.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema (layered or microhyphema)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior chamber cells and flare</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iris sphincter tears</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iridodialysis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens subluxation&#x000a0;or dislocation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic cataract</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal blood staining</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These findings help determine the mechanism of IOP elevation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Gonioscopy (Critical Diagnostic Step)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Gonioscopy is mandatory&#x000a0;in all traumatic glaucoma evaluations.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 7. Key Gonioscopic Findings</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Finding</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Interpretation</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Widened ciliary body band</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Irregular angle structures</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ciliary body tear</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Secondary angle closure</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trabecular pigmentation</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prior hemorrhage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cyclodialysis cleft</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Separation of the ciliary body</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle scarring</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Penetrating injury</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Angle recession greater than 180&#x000b0; significantly increases the risk of long-term glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Optic Nerve Assessment </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Fundoscopy:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cup-to-disc ratio</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rim thinning</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Notching</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disc hemorrhages</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Asymmetry</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma typically presents unilaterally.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Optical coherence tomography:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal nerve fiber layer thinning</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ganglion cell complex loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Asymmetry with the fellow eye</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>OCT detects early structural damage before visual field loss.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual Field Testing</bold>
</p>
        <p>Standard automated perimetry evaluates functional damage. Common findings include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasal step</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Arcuate scotoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Paracentral defects</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Generalized depression in advanced cases</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Repeated testing is essential for progression monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Anterior Segment Imaging </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ultrasound biomicroscopy:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Detects angle recession</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Identifies cyclodialysis cleft</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluates ciliary body damage</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Anterior segment optical&#x000a0;coherence&#x000a0;tomography:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Noncontact angle assessment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluates angle narrowing or scarring&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r30">[30]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Posterior Segment Imaging B-Scan Ultrasonography</bold>
</p>
        <p>Indicated when media opacity prevents visualization:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Vitreous hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal detachment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Choroidal effusion</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Special Situations </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyphema evaluation:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor IOP daily</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluate for rebleeding (3-7 days post-trauma)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Check corneal staining&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r84">[84]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Ghost cell glaucoma:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>History of vitreous hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Khaki-colored cells in the anterior chamber&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r85">[85]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Retained intraocular foreign body suspicion:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Computed tomography orbit (preferred)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid magnetic resonance imaging if a metallic foreign body is suspected</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Not routinely required unless:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Penetrating trauma with suspected intraocular foreign bodies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Systemic steroid therapy planned</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Associated systemic injuries&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r86">[86]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Risk Stratification</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients at high risk require long-term follow-up:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Extensive angle recession (&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevated IOP at presentation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema with rebleeding</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens dislocation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>History of penetrating trauma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Follow-up intervals should be individualized, often lifelong.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Diagnostic Algorithm</bold>
</p>
        <p>History of Ocular Trauma&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Measure IOP&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Perform Slit-Lamp Exam&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Mandatory Gonioscopy&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Angle Recession Present?&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Yes &#x02192; Long-term surveillance&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Assess Optic Nerve (OCT + VF)&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Stage and Treat Accordingly</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 8. Diagnostic Components in Traumatic Glaucoma</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Diagnostic Tool</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Purpose</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Key Findings</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tonometry</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Detect elevated IOP</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Asymmetry&#x000a0;and spikes</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Slit-lamp examination</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Identify anterior segment damage</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema and inflammation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Gonioscopy</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Evaluate angle integrity</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>OCT RNFL</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Structural optic nerve damage</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Thinning</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Visual field</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Functional loss</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Scotomas</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>UBM</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ciliary body damage</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clefts and tears</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CT orbit</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Detect IOFB</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Metallic fragments</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: IOP, intraocular pressure; OCT, optical coherence tomography; RNFL, retinal nerve fiber layer; UBM, ultrasound biomicroscopy; CT, computed tomography; IOFB, intraocular foreign body.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Evaluation Principles</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy is mandatory in all traumatic cases.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession may be missed without careful comparison to the fellow eye.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic glaucoma is typically unilateral.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong follow-up is necessary in extensive angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Imaging supports diagnosis but does not replace clinical examination.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Evaluation of traumatic glaucoma requires a comprehensive, mechanism-driven approach that incorporates a detailed history, tonometry, slit-lamp examination, mandatory gonioscopy, optic nerve assessment, and appropriate imaging. Early detection of angle damage and optic neuropathy is essential to prevent irreversible vision loss. Because delayed presentation is common, structured long-term monitoring remains a cornerstone of management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s10" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Closed-Globe Trauma</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Early-onset:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic hyphema
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Initial management of hyphema involves medical therapy, including topical corticosteroids and cycloplegia. Strict bed rest with a 45&#x000b0; head elevation is advised to allow blood to settle inferiorly, thereby clearing the visual axis. Protection of the globe with a rigid plastic eye shield is advised to help prevent further ocular trauma. Antiplatelet and anticoagulation agents,&#x000a0;as well as nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, should be considered for withholding in consultation with the patient's physician.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Certain high-risk cases may require hospital admission for close observation. Such cases include patients who may be non-compliant with their treatment regimen, children, rebleeding cases, Grade II hyphemas or worse, and those with sickle cell trait or disease. Rebleeds can occur 2&#x000a0;to 5 days after the initial injury, and patients should be closely monitored in the early stages.</p></list-item><list-item><p>If the IOP is&#x000a0;elevated and treatment is indicated, several factors should be considered, including the status of the optic disc, the presence of preexisting glaucoma, central corneal thickness, and the likelihood of IOP elevation settling. When treatment is required, a stepwise approach is recommended, starting with topical ocular antihypertensive medications. First-line treatment typically includes a topical beta-blocker, an alpha-2 agonist, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, or a combination of these agents. Although clinical practice patterns vary, some clinicians avoid prostaglandin analogs because they are theoretically proinflammatory and may further disrupt the blood&#x02013;aqueous barrier, exacerbating intraocular inflammation and potentially increasing IOP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r87">[87]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Some clinics also use prostaglandin analogs in these situations. If the IOP remains inadequately controlled with topical treatment alone, oral or intravenous acetazolamide may be considered. The use of both topical and systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, as well as osmotic agents such as mannitol, should be avoided in patients with sickle cell trait or disease because these medications can promote sickling due to acidosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r88">[88]</xref> If the IOP is acutely elevated, treatment with multiple agents may be initiated simultaneously, with the expectation that medications can later be withdrawn in a stepwise manner.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Surgical intervention, such as anterior chamber paracentesis and anterior chamber washout to evacuate the hyphema, may be needed in certain cases if medical treatment fails. A patient with high IOP that is resistant to maximum medical therapy should be a candidate for surgery. A general guideline&#x000a0;proposed by Wilson indicates that&#x000a0;individuals&#x000a0;with IOP greater than 50 mmHg for over 5 days, IOP greater than 45 mmHg for over&#x000a0;1 week, or IOP greater than 35 mmHg for over&#x000a0;2 weeks should be considered for surgery. A more cautious approach is recommended in patients with sickle cell trait or disease, ie, IOP greater than 24 mmHg for over&#x000a0;1 day.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r88">[88]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Other criteria include persistent grade IV hyphema, also called eight-ball hyphema, and early corneal blood staining. Despite an anticipated high failure rate and a short duration of drainage, trabeculectomy has been described in this context as a means to maintain a safe IOP. At the same time, the hyphema clears, and aqueous outflow normalizes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r89">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r90">[90]</xref>&#x000a0;However, some prefer glaucoma drainage devices such as an Ahmed or Baerveldt Tube Shunt to achieve longer-term IOP control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r91">[91]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic iritis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Topical corticosteroids and cycloplegia are the mainstays of treatment. In these cases, an elevated IOP is typically self-limiting, and topical ocular antihypertensives are generally sufficient&#x000a0;for control.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabecular meshwork injury
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Topical ocular antihypertensives are typically sufficient to counteract the initial rise in IOP. The use of lasers, such as argon laser trabeculoplasty or selective laser trabeculoplasty, applied to the angle may also be considered. Kelman et al and Akkin et al previously reported using argon laser trabeculoplasty directed to the traumatized Schlemm's canal to treat recurrent hyphema due to laceration to the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm's canal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r92">[92]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r93">[93]</xref> Late-onset IOP elevation may occur due to scarring of the angle structures and may require incisional glaucoma surgery.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Treatment of raised IOP in a well-established suprachoroidal hemorrhage is initially medical with topical and systemic ocular antihypertensives. Topical corticosteroids reduce intraocular inflammation and improve comfort. Cycloplegic agents rotate the ciliary body posteriorly and may help to maintain anterior chamber depth and minimize angle closure. Cycloplegia also reduces ciliary spasms and may help to relieve some of the ocular pain commonly associated with suprachoroidal hemorrhage. Surgical drainage should be considered if a high IOP or severe pain remains persistent. A consensus on the decision and optimal timing for drainage has not yet been established, but in principle, waiting 7 to 14 days for clot liquefaction is preferable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r94">[94]</xref>&#x000a0;Indications for more urgent surgical drainage include kissing choroidal, retinal detachment, and persistently high IOP.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Late-onset:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid response
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Patients with a previously known steroid response and predisposing risk factors, including those with high myopia, primary open-angle glaucoma or glaucoma suspects, first-degree relatives with primary open-angle glaucoma, type I diabetes, connective tissue disease (especially men with rheumatoid arthritis), and angle recession, should be closely monitored when initiating corticosteroid therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r95">[95]</xref>&#x000a0;The effect of a steroid-induced IOP elevation is normally temporary and reversible if corticosteroids are used for less than 12 months. However, more than 18 months of corticosteroid use may lead to long-term IOP elevation that persists even after therapy withdrawal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r95">[95]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>For persistent inflammation, lower-potency corticosteroids with reduced ocular penetration should be considered. If periocular depot corticosteroids were used, they may be surgically excised.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;If a steroid response occurs after intravitreal injection, such as triamcinolone acetonide, removal through vitrectomy can be helpful.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r96">[96]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>The management of IOP elevation follows the same stepwise approach as in primary open-angle glaucoma. However, evidence suggests that selective laser trabeculoplasty in these patients is more effective at lowering IOP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r97">[97]</xref>&#x000a0;Goniotomy and canaloplasty can be considered, with 2 small case series showing encouraging results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r99">[99]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In cases of angle recession greater than 180&#x000b0;, a lifelong annual examination is required to detect glaucoma, as it may develop several months to years after the initial traumatic insult.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Topical ocular antihypertensives&#x000a0;are the mainstay of treatment. Beta-blockers, alpha-agonists, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are effective in angle recession glaucoma. Some practitioners believe that prostaglandin analogs should be used cautiously because of their theoretical proinflammatory effects.&#x000a0;Other practitioners use them routinely but consider withdrawing them or substituting with another drug class if intraocular inflammation is persistent or other considerations arise, such as cystoid macular edema. These&#x000a0;prostaglandin analogs also have a theoretical benefit, as they reduce IOP via the uveoscleral pathway, thereby bypassing the injured trabecular meshwork.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Argon laser trabeculoplasty, an outdated treatment modality, was found to have a minimal therapeutic effect in angle recession glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r100">[100]</xref>&#x000a0;Trabeculopuncture with an Nd:YAG laser has previously been described; a small study with short follow-up showed some IOP-lowering effect compared to argon laser trabeculoplasty.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r101">[101]</xref>&#x000a0;Non-enhanced trabeculectomies have a high failure rate, with posttraumatic angle recession an independent risk factor. In contrast, a study has shown that trabeculectomy with mitomycin-C yields favorable results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r102">[102]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r103">[103]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens subluxation and dislocation
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>If a pupil block is present or suspected to occur intermittently, a laser peripheral iridotomy can be performed to relieve or prevent it.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacomorphic glaucoma&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Initial treatment is with topical and systemic ocular antihypertensives. Laser iridotomy may be considered to treat any component of angle closure suspected to be secondary to relative pupil block. Evidence shows that immediate argon laser peripheral iridoplasty is a safe and effective first-line treatment for acute phacomorphic angle closure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r104">[104]</xref>&#x000a0;However, these patients often require surgical removal of the lens for definitive treatment of the phacomorphic component of their angle closure.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phacolytic glaucoma, lens particle glaucoma, and phacoantigenic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The management of phacolytic glaucoma follows the same stepwise approach as initial medical management, including frequent topical steroids and cycloplegia. Ultimately, lens extraction is required, ideally once intraocular inflammation has been controlled or stabilized, although this is not always achievable. Often, these patients develop peripheral anterior synechiae, and lens surgery may be combined with goniosynechialysis. Combined cataract surgery and trabeculectomy has been described, but it is generally recommended&#x000a0;due to the high rate of trabeculectomy failure. The management of lens particle glaucoma and phacoantigenic glaucoma is very similar.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r105">[105]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r106">[106]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ghost cell glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Ghost cell glaucoma often responds to medical treatment. However, anterior chamber washout and pars plana vitrectomy may be considered in the setting of dense vitreous hemorrhage or recalcitrant high IOP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r59">[59]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemolytic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Treatment is similar to that in ghost cell glaucoma, starting with conservative medical therapy, followed by anterior chamber washout and pars plana vitrectomy if needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r107">[107]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemosiderotic glaucoma
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The primary principle in the management of hemosiderotic glaucoma is the surgical removal of the iron-containing intraocular foreign body. The literature on hemosiderotic glaucoma is sparse; previous studies have primarily focused on the histological findings from enucleated eyes.&#x000a0;A case report described normalization of IOP following removal of the foreign body.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r108">[108]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Carotid-cavernous sinus fistula
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Direct high-flow carotid-cavernous sinus fistula can be treated using endovascular interventional techniques with embolic materials such as detachable balloons, coils, and acrylic glue to occlude the fistulous connection. Indirect low-flow fistula can be managed with observation, as up to 70% of these fistulae have been shown to close spontaneously.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Other options include intermittent compression of the ipsilateral internal carotid artery or superior ophthalmic vein, stereotactic radiosurgery, and endovascular surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r1">[1]</xref> Elevated IOP may be managed temporarily with topical and systemic ocular antihypertensives, as in other forms of glaucoma.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Open-Globe Trauma</bold>
<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Epithelial downgrowth:&#x000a0;</bold>Different treatment modalities may be considered depending on the type of epithelial downgrowth. In general, epithelial pearls and cysts can be monitored if they are not otherwise causing an elevation in IOP. If they are growing in size and causing complications such as inflammation or IOP elevation, an en bloc surgical resection can be considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r109">[109]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r110">[110]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r111">[111]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Short-term success has been reported with surgical resection of epithelial membranes, often combined with cryotherapy and intraocular 5-fluorouracil injection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r112">[112]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r113">[113]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r114">[114]</xref>&#x000a0;However, these measures tend to have a high failure rate in the medium to long term.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r115">[115]</xref>&#x000a0;Glaucoma drainage tube surgery&#x000a0;is the first-line surgical option for elevated IOP that persists despite maximally tolerated medical therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r116">[116]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Filtering surgery often fails because the ostium becomes obstructed by epithelial cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r117">[117]</xref>&#x000a0;If IOP remains uncontrolled following all these measures, cycloablation can be considered as a last resort.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Fibrous downgrowth:&#x000a0;</bold>Similar to epithelial downgrowth, filtering surgery has high failure rates in fibrous downgrowth. Glaucoma drainage tube implantation in this setting may not work due to blockage of the tube lumen by fibrovascular tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r116">[116]</xref>&#x000a0;Surgical removal of the tissue can be attempted. Combined intracorneal and subconjunctival bevacizumab injection has been reported to arrest further vascularization of the fibrovascular membrane and may also control intraocular hemorrhage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r63">[63]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s11" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Since&#x000a0;traumatic glaucoma is an umbrella term for all the different causes of IOP elevation secondary to trauma, one should consider all the various etiologies outlined within traumatic glaucoma as part of their differential diagnoses to target the treatment.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table8" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 9.&#x000a0;Differential Diagnosis Based on Mechanism of IOP Elevation</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 798px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Condition</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Etiology/Mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Key Clinical Features</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Gonioscopy Findings</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Distinguishing Features</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tear between ciliary muscle fibers after blunt ocular&#x000a0;trauma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Often delayed IOP elevation; unilateral</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Widened ciliary body band</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>History of trauma and asymmetry with the fellow eye</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Primary open-angle glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Idiopathic trabecular dysfunction</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Bilateral and gradual progression</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Normal open angles</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>No trauma history</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema-associated glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>RBC obstruction of the trabecular meshwork</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Layered blood in AC</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Open angle with blood</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute after trauma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ghost cell glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Degenerated RBCs from vitreous hemorrhage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Khaki-colored cells</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Open angle and cells present</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Occurs weeks after VH</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Phacolytic glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lens protein leakage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Mature cataract and inflammation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Open angle with macrophages</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyper-mature lens</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Phacoanaphylactic glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Immune response to lens proteins</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Granulomatous inflammation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Open angle</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lens capsule rupture</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroid-induced glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroid use post-trauma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Gradual IOP elevation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Open angles</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>History of steroid therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Inflammatory (uveitic) glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trauma-induced uveitis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>AC cells or flare</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>PAS or open angle</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Active inflammation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Synechial closure from inflammation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive angle closure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>PAS visible</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic inflammation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cyclodialysis cleft closure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rebound IOP after hypotony</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prior low IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cleft visible</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>History of hypotony</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lens subluxation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pupillary block</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Irregular pupil</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Narrow angle</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lens displacement</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOFB-related glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Metal ion toxicity</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Iris heterochromia</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pigmented TM</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Imaging confirms IOFB</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neovascular glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Retinal ischemia</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rubeosis iridis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neovascularization</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Diabetes&#x000a0;mellitus or CRVO history</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute angle closure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pupillary block</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Severe pain, mid-dilated pupil</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Closed angle</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>No trauma history typically</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 129px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hemolytic glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 162px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>RBC breakdown products</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151.734px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Red cells in AC</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 136.266px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Open angle</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 153px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>After intraocular bleeding</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: AC, anterior chamber; CRVO, central retinal vein occlusion; IOFB, intraocular foreign body; IOP, intraocular pressure; PAS, peripheral anterior synechiae; RBCs, red blood cells; TM, trabecular meshwork; VH, vitreous hemorrhage.</p>
