        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table11" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 12.&#x000a0;Pediatric Differential Diagnosis</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 290.12px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Condition</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 281.88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Differentiating Feature</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 290.12px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Congenital glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 281.88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Bilateral and buphthalmos</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 290.12px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Juvenile open-angle glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 281.88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>No trauma history</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 290.12px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Traumatic hyphema</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 281.88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clear injury history</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 290.12px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lens subluxation (Marfan)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 281.88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Systemic signs</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Key Points in Differentiation</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic glaucoma is typically unilateral.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy is essential for diagnosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The history of ocular trauma is critical but may be remote.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema suggests an acute mechanism.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed IOP rise suggests angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intraocular foreign bodies should be ruled out with imaging when suspected.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid-induced IOP rise must always be considered.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table12" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;13. Diagnostic Clue Summary</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 597px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 291.354px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Finding</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 279.646px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Most Likely Diagnosis</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 291.354px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Widened ciliary body band</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 279.646px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 291.354px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Khaki-colored cells</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 279.646px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ghost cell glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 291.354px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyper-mature cataract and inflammation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 279.646px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Phacolytic glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 291.354px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Iris neovascularization</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 279.646px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Neovascular glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 291.354px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Low IOP then rebound</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 279.646px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cyclodialysis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation:&#x000a0;IOP, intraocular pressure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s12" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma lacks large, disease-specific randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparable to those for primary open-angle glaucoma. Most management recommendations are derived from observational cohorts, retrospective surgical series, trauma registries, and extrapolation from major glaucoma trials. Nevertheless, several key studies and ongoing clinical research provide the evidence base for current practice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Evidence Supporting IOP Reduction in Secondary Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although not trauma-specific, landmark glaucoma trials establish the principle that lowering IOP reduces the risk of optic nerve damage progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table13" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;14. Major Foundational Trials</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 228.362px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Study</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 259.638px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Relevance to Traumatic Glaucoma</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 270px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Key Outcome</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 228.362px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ocular Hypertension Treatment Study</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 259.638px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Supports early treatment of elevated IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 270px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>20%-25% IOP reduction lowers the glaucoma conversion</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 228.362px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early Manifest Glaucoma Trial</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 259.638px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Demonstrates the benefit of IOP lowering</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 270px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>A 1 mmHg IOP reduction decreases progression risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 228.362px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Advanced Glaucoma Intervention Study</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 259.638px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Importance of sustained IOP control</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 270px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Better visual outcomes with lower mean IOP</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: IOP, intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>These trials underpin the universal recommendation for aggressive IOP control in traumatic glaucoma once optic nerve damage or sustained elevation is detected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r118">[118]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Studies on Angle Recession Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Angle recession is the most common delayed form of traumatic glaucoma. Key observations from cohort studies include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Up to 50%&#x000a0;to 60% of eyes with extensive (&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;) angle recession develop elevated IOP over time.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed onset may occur 5 to 20 years after the initial trauma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greater circumferential angle damage correlates with a higher risk of glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic nerve damage parallels chronic open-angle glaucoma patterns.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Longitudinal observational studies recommend lifelong surveillance for patients with significant angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r119">[119]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyphema Management Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinical research on traumatic hyphema provides evidence for IOP management. Findings from trauma cohorts include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Elevated IOP occurs in 5% to 40% of hyphema cases.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rebleeding increases the risk of permanent optic nerve damage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early IOP control reduces long-term glaucomatous changes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical washout recommended when IOP &#x0003e;50 mmHg for &#x0003e;5 days (adult data).</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These findings support prompt IOP-lowering therapy and monitoring in acute traumatic cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r120">[120]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Evidence in Traumatic Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Because traumatic glaucoma may be resistant to medical therapy, surgical studies are particularly relevant.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Trabeculectomy studies:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lower success rates compared to primary open-angle glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher risk of scarring due to post-traumatic inflammation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adjunctive mitomycin C improves outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r121">[121]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Glaucoma drainage devices:</bold> Multiple retrospective series suggest that:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts have higher long-term success than trabeculectomy in traumatic glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Particularly effective in cases with angle scarring or peripheral anterior synechiae.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Preferred in young patients with significant structural damage.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Minimally invasive glaucoma surgery:</bold>&#x000a0;Emerging evidence suggests that:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Minimally invasive glaucoma surgery may be less effective in extensive angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabecular bypass procedures depend on intact distal outflow pathways.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Limited but growing evidence in selected mild cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r70">[70]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular Foreign Bodies and Metal Toxicity Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinical case series demonstrate the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early removal of iron-containing foreign bodies reduces the risk of siderosis-related glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed removal correlates with progressive trabecular damage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Iron deposition causes oxidative injury and fibrosis.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These findings support early surgical intervention for the removal of intraocular foreign bodies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r122">[122]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Steroid Response Evidence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Studies on steroid-induced ocular hypertension show the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Approximately 5% to 30% of patients are moderate responders.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trauma patients receiving prolonged corticosteroids are at increased risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP monitoring during steroid therapy is essential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r123">[123]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Paediatric Traumatic Glaucoma Evidence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Paediatric cohort studies show the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher inflammatory response after trauma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greater risk of amblyopia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Variable surgical outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early detection improves visual prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r124">[124]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Ongoing Research and Emerging Directions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although few RCTs specifically target traumatic glaucoma, ongoing research includes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anti-fibrotic agents for investigating improved wound modulation after glaucoma surgery in secondary glaucomas.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neuroprotection to&#x000a0;explore agents that prevent optic nerve degeneration beyond IOP control.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Biomarkers of fibrosis for studying TGF-&#x003b2; signaling and extracellular matrix remodeling in posttraumatic angle scarring.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advanced imaging studies using OCT angiography and UBM to detect early angle damage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Novel glaucoma drainage devices to&#x000a0;refine tube shunt materials and placement techniques for secondary glaucomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r125">[125]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table14" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;15.&#x000a0;Summary of Evidence-Based Recommendations</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical Scenario</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Evidence-Based Approach</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elevated IOP post-trauma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Immediate IOP reduction</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Extensive angle recession</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lifelong monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema with high IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Aggressive medical treatment with or without surgical therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Failed medical therapy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prefer tube shunt over trabeculectomy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOFB present</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early removal</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 218.208px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroid use</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 351.792px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Close IOP monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: IOP, intraocular pressure; IOFB, intraocular foreign body.<bold>&#x000a0;</bold></p>
        <p>
<bold>Evidence Strength Overview</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Strong evidence supports IOP reduction to prevent optic neuropathy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate evidence supports tube shunts in secondary glaucomas.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Observational evidence supports lifelong surveillance in angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Limited RCT data are available specific to traumatic glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Although traumatic glaucoma lacks disease-specific RCTs, management recommendations are supported by a combination of trauma cohort studies, secondary glaucoma surgical outcomes, and foundational glaucoma trials. The consistent finding across all studies is that sustained IOP reduction significantly improves visual outcomes. Surgical intervention&#x02014;particularly glaucoma drainage devices&#x02014;demonstrates favorable long-term success in refractory cases. Ongoing research focuses on fibrosis modulation, neuroprotection, and improved diagnostic imaging to enhance early detection and long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r126">[126]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s13" sec-type="Treatment Planning ">
        <title>Treatment Planning </title>
        <p>Treatment planning for traumatic glaucoma is mechanism-based and individualized. The approach depends on the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Timing (acute versus chronic)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mechanism of IOP elevation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severity of optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of inflammation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Structural angle damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patient age and comorbidities</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The overarching goal is to reduce IOP to prevent progressive optic neuropathy and address the underlying cause.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Acute Management Planning</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Elevated IOP following hyphema:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Medical therapy
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Topical beta-blockers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Alpha-agonists</p></list-item><list-item><p>Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</p></list-item><list-item><p>Systemic acetazolamide (if needed)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Avoid
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Prostaglandin analogues in active inflammation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Miotics in hyphema</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>IOP &#x0003e;50 mmHg for &#x0003e;5 days</p></list-item><list-item><p>Total hyphema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal blood staining</p></list-item><list-item><p>Optic nerve compromise</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Anterior chamber washout may be required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r128">[128]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Inflammatory (uveitic) mechanism:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Topical corticosteroids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cycloplegics</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP-lowering agents</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gradual tapering to avoid rebound</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Steroid responders require close monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r129">[129]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Lens-related mechanisms:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>If pupillary block: Laser peripheral iridotomy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If lens subluxation: Lens extraction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If phacolytic glaucoma: Cataract extraction&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r130">[130]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Chronic Angle Recession Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Angle recession glaucoma is the most common delayed presentation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Stepwise approach: </bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>First-line&#x000a0;therapy
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Topical prostaglandin analogues (if no active inflammation)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Beta-blockers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</p></list-item><list-item><p>Alpha-agonists</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Second-line&#x000a0;therapy
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Combination therapy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Oral acetazolamide (short-term)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Medical therapy may be less effective due to structural trabecular damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r128">[128]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Laser Treatment Planning </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Selective laser trabeculoplasty:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>May be considered in mild angle recession</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Effectiveness is reduced if extensive trabecular scarring</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Less predictable than in primary open-angle glaucoma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r131">[131]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Peripheral iridotomy:&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indicated if the pupillary block mechanism is present.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Treatment Planning</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical planning depends on the integrity of the angle and the presence of fibrosis.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Trabeculectomy indications:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Failed medical therapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Considerations
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Higher failure rate due to scarring</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mitomycin C recommended</p></list-item><list-item><p>Careful wound modulation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r132">[132]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Glaucoma drainage devices (tube shunts):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Often preferred in traumatic glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Extensive angle recession</p></list-item><list-item><p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p></list-item><list-item><p>Failed trabeculectomy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Young patients</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Advantages
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Bypass damaged trabecular meshwork</p></list-item><list-item><p>Higher long-term success in secondary glaucoma</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Common devices
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Ahmed valve</p></list-item><list-item><p>Baerveldt implant&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r133">[133]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Minimally invasive glaucoma surgery:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Limited role in extensive angle damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>May be considered in mild cases</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Requires intact distal outflow&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r134">[134]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Cyclodestructive procedures:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Reserved for
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Refractory glaucoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Poor visual potential</p></list-item><list-item><p>Advanced optic nerve damage</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Methods
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Transscleral cyclophotocoagulation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Endoscopic cyclophotocoagulation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r135">[135]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Special Clinical Scenarios</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular foreign body-associated glaucoma:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Immediate foreign body removal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Address metal toxicity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>May require combined glaucoma surgery</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Paediatric traumatic glaucoma:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>More aggressive inflammatory response</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher surgical failure rate</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong follow-up required&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r136">[136]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Target IOP Determination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Target IOP depends on:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severity of optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Baseline IOP</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Age</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fellow eye status&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r137">[137]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 16. </bold>Recommended&#x000a0;Target IOP&#x000a0;Based on Glaucoma Severity</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table15" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35.176px;">
                <td style="height: 35.176px; width: 229.742px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.176px; width: 341.258px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Recommended Target IOP</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 229.742px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 341.258px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;18 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 229.742px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 341.258px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;15 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 229.742px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Advanced</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 341.258px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;12 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: IOP,&#x000a0;Intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Follow-Up and Monitoring Plan</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early stage
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Every 3-4 months</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>After surgery
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Frequent early monitoring</p></list-item><list-item><p>Watch for hypotony, bleb failure</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Long-term
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Lifelong annual gonioscopy</p></list-item><list-item><p>OCT and visual field monitoring</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment Algorithm</bold>
</p>
        <p>History of Trauma&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Measure IOP&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Identify Mechanism&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Acute Cause? &#x02192; Treat Specific Mechanism&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Chronic Angle Damage?&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Medical Therapy&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Failure?&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Tube Shunt Preferred&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 17. </bold>Treatment Strategy by Mechanism</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table16" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>First-Line Treatment</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Surgical Option if Needed</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Medical + washout if needed</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>AC washout</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Topical meds</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tube shunt</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lens-induced</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cataract extraction</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Combined surgery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Inflammatory</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroids + IOP meds</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trabeculectomy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOFB</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Removal</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tube shunt</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cyclodialysis closure</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Medical</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical repair</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: AC, anterior chamber; IOP, intraocular pressure; IOFB, intraocular foreign body.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Treatment Principles</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Treat the underlying mechanism.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid overtreatment during the hypotony phase.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor steroid response.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts often outperform trabeculectomy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong surveillance is required in angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Treatment planning in traumatic glaucoma is individualized and mechanism-driven. Acute IOP elevation requires immediate control, while chronic angle recession glaucoma often necessitates long-term therapy or surgical intervention. Glaucoma drainage devices are frequently preferred because they address structural damage to the angle and have higher success rates. Early recognition and sustained IOP reduction remain critical to preserving visual function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s14" sec-type="Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management">
        <title>Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management</title>
        <p>Management of traumatic glaucoma involves prolonged medical therapy, laser procedures, and surgical interventions, all of which carry potential ocular and systemic adverse effects. Early identification and proactive management of these toxicities are essential to preserve vision and prevent further optic nerve damage.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Therapy&#x02013;Related Toxicity</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Topical beta-blockers (timolol):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Dry eye</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal anesthesia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Allergic blepharoconjunctivitis</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Systemic effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Bradycardia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypotension</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bronchospasm</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fatigue</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Avoid use in patients with asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and heart block</p></list-item><list-item><p>Switch to a selective beta-1 blocker (betaxolol)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Use punctal occlusion to reduce systemic absorption&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r138">[138]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Prostaglandin analogues (latanoprost and bimatoprost):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Conjunctival hyperemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Iris pigmentation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Periocular skin darkening</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cystoid macular edema (especially post-trauma)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Exacerbation of uveitis</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Avoid active inflammation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Monitor the macula in posttraumatic cases</p></list-item><list-item><p>Discontinue if cystoid macular edema develops&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r139">[139]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Alpha-agonists (brimonidine):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Allergic follicular conjunctivitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ocular redness</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Systemic effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Drowsiness</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypotension (especially in children)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Avoid use in pediatric patients</p></list-item><list-item><p>Discontinue if an allergy develops&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r140">[140]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Topical (dorzolamide)
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Corneal edema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bitter taste</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral (acetazolamide)
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Paresthesia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Metabolic acidosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypokalemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal stones</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Monitor electrolytes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Avoid use in patients with renal impairment</p></list-item><list-item><p>Short-term use preferred&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r141">[141]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Steroids (used in inflammatory mechanisms):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Steroid-induced IOP rise</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cataract progression</p></list-item><list-item><p>Delayed wound healing</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Taper appropriately</p></list-item><list-item><p>Monitor IOP closely</p></list-item><list-item><p>Switch to loteprednol in steroid responders<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r142">[142]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Laser Treatment&#x02013;Related Complications </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Selective laser trabeculoplasty:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Transient IOP spike</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inflammation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Peripheral anterior synechiae</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Prophylactic apraclonidine</p></list-item><list-item><p>Short-course nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or steroids</p></list-item><list-item><p>Monitor IOP within 1-2 hours post-procedure<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r143">[143]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Toxicity and Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical intervention carries the highest risk of adverse events.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Trabeculectomy complications:</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 18.</bold>
</bold>Early and Late Surgical Complications of Trabeculectomy</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table17" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 293.846px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Early</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 276.154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Late</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 293.846px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hypotony</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 276.154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Bleb leak</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 293.846px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Shallow AC</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 276.154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Bleb infection</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 293.846px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Choroidal effusion</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 276.154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Endophthalmitis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 293.846px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 276.154px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Late hypotony</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: AC,&#x000a0;anterior&#x000a0;chamber.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Tight suturing</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anterior chamber reformation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bleb revision if needed</p></list-item><list-item><p>Prophylactic antibiotics&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r144">[144]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Glaucoma drainage devices:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypotony</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tube occlusion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal touch</p></list-item><list-item><p>Diplopia</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Late complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Tube exposure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Endophthalmitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal decompensation</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Proper tube positioning</p></list-item><list-item><p>Patch graft coverage</p></list-item><list-item><p>Surgical revision if exposure occurs&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r145">[145]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Cyclodestructive procedures:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Chronic hypotony</p></list-item><list-item><p>Phthisis bulbi</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inflammation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vision loss</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x000a0;Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Reserved for refractory cases</p></list-item><list-item><p>Conservative energy settings</p></list-item><list-item><p>Close monitoring&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r146">[146]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Complications Specific to Trauma Context</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyphema rebleed:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Occurs 25 days post-injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased risk of optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Strict head elevation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Avoid nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</p></list-item><list-item><p>Monitor daily</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Angle recession progression:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Late progressive optic neuropathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Often asymptomatic initially</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Lifelong monitoring</p></list-item><list-item><p>Annual gonioscopy</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Intraocular foreign body&#x02013;related toxicity:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Siderosis (iron)
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Iris heterochromia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal degeneration</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Chalcosis (copper)
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Kayser-Fleischer&#x02013;like deposits</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Management
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Early intraocular foreign body removal</p></list-item><list-item><p>ERG monitoring&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r147">[147]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic and Psychological Impact</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma patients may experience:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PTSD (in assault cases)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Depression due to vision loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occupational impairment</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Management:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Multidisciplinary care</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychological counseling referral</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low vision rehabilitation<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r148">[148]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table18" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 19. Summary of Toxicities and Management</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Intervention</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Major Risk</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Monitoring Strategy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Preventive Measure</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Beta-blockers</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Bradycardia</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pulse, BP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Avoid in&#x000a0;patients with cardiac disease</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prostaglandins</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CME</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>OCT macula</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Avoid in patients with active inflammation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroids</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOP rise</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Frequent IOP checks</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Taper carefully</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>SLT</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOP spike</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Immediate post-laser IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Apraclonidine</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trabeculectomy</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hypotony</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early postoperative visits</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Controlled filtration</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tube shunt</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tube exposure</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Slit-lamp examination</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Patch graft</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Cyclophotocoagulation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.781px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Phthisis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 180.219px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOP&#x000a0;and visual acuity</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Conservative dosing</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: BP, blood pressure; CME, cystoid macular edema; IOP, intraocular pressure; OCT, optical coherence tomography; SLT, selective laser trabeculoplasty.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Risk Stratification in Traumatic Glaucoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>The risk of complications is higher in the following situations:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Young patients</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Extensive angle recession</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe inflammation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prior multiple surgeries</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid responders</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Surveillance Strategy</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP every 3&#x000a0;to 6 months</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>OCT retinal nerve fiber layer annually</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual field testing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy yearly</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor fellow eye</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Key Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts are often safer than trabeculectomy in trauma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostaglandin analogues should be avoided during active inflammation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypotony can be more dangerous than elevated IOP.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid response must always be considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r149">[149]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s15" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma does not have a universally accepted staging system specific to trauma; however, staging is clinically essential for guiding treatment intensity, determining target IOP, and predicting prognosis. In practice, staging is based on the following factors:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severity of optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual field loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Degree of angle damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Level of IOP elevation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Structural progression</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Because traumatic glaucoma often evolves over time&#x02014;especially in cases of angle recession&#x02014;both mechanistic staging (based on injury type) and functional staging (based on glaucomatous damage) are clinically relevant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Mechanism-Based Clinical Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>This staging categorizes traumatic glaucoma according to pathophysiologic phase.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table19" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 20. Mechanistic Staging of Traumatic Glaucoma</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 795px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Phase</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Pathophysiology</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Clinical Features</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Management Focus</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stage 0</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ocular trauma without IOP elevation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Structural damage without outflow compromise</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Normal IOP; possible angle recession</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Observation and long-term monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stage 1</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute IOP elevation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyphema, inflammation, and RBC obstruction</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pain, corneal edema, and visible hyphema</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Medical IOP control; treat the cause</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stage 2</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Subacute inflammatory phase</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trabeculitis and PAS formation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>AC cells&#x000a0;or flare; variable IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Anti-inflammatory&#x000a0;and IOP therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stage 3</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic structural damage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Angle recession and trabecular fibrosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Persistent elevated IOP</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Long-term medical therapy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stage 4</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Established traumatic glaucoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Optic nerve damage&#x000a0;and field loss</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>RNFL thinning and VF defects</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Aggressive IOP lowering</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 70px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stage 5</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 151px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Refractory/advanced disease</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Extensive angle damage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 156.146px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive vision loss</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.854px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical intervention</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Reference for the table.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <p>Abbreviations: AC, anterior chamber; IOP, intraocular pressure; PAS, peripheral anterior synechiae; RBC, red blood cell; RNFL, retinal nerve fiber layer; VF, visual field.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Functional Staging (Based on Optic Nerve and Visual Field Damage)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma ultimately follows standard glaucomatous staging criteria once optic neuropathy develops.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table20" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 21. Severity Staging Based on Visual Field and Optic Nerve</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 89px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.555px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Optic Nerve Findings</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.445px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Visual Field Defect</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 133px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Cup-to-Disc Ratio</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Suggested Target IOP</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 89px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Suspect</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.555px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Normal disc; asymmetry</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.445px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Normal</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 133px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;0.6</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;21 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 89px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Mild</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.555px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Focal rim thinning</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.445px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Paracentral defect</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 133px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>0.6-0.7</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;18 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 89px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.555px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rim loss</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.445px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Arcuate scotoma</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 133px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>0.7-0.8</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;15 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 89px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Severe</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.555px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Advanced rim loss</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.445px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Central field threatened</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 133px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e;0.8</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;12 mmHg</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 89px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>End-stage</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.555px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Near-total cupping</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 171.445px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tunnel vision</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 133px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e;0.9</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Very low IOP required</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: IOP, intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Angle Recession&#x02013;Specific Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>Angle recession severity correlates with the risk of delayed glaucoma.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table21" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 22. Angle Recession Severity Classification</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 250.419px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Extent of Angle Damage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 321.581px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Risk of Developing Glaucoma</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 250.419px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;90&#x000b0;</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 321.581px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Low</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 250.419px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>90-180&#x000b0;</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 321.581px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 250.419px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 321.581px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>High</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 250.419px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>360&#x000b0;</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 321.581px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Very high; lifelong risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>A greater than 180&#x000b0; recession significantly increases the risk of long-term glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hyphema-Associated Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>In acute traumatic hyphema, staging is based on the blood level.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table22" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 23. Hyphema Grading</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 231.565px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Grade</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 339.435px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Blood in Anterior Chamber</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 231.565px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Grade 0</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 339.435px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Microhyphema (circulating RBCs)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 231.565px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Grade I</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 339.435px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;1/3 chamber filled</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 231.565px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Grade II</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 339.435px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>1/3-1/2 filled</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 231.565px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Grade III</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 339.435px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e;1/2 filled</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 231.565px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Grade IV</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 339.435px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Total hyphema (eight-ball hyphema)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: RBCs, red blood cells.</p>
        <p>Higher grades increase the risk of optic nerve damage and corneal blood staining.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r151">[151]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Structural OCT-Based Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>OCT allows earlier staging before visual field loss.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table23" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;24.&#x000a0;Optical Coherence Tomography&#x02013;Based Staging of Traumatic Glaucoma</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 262.594px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>RNFL Thickness</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 308.406px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Interpretation</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 262.594px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003e;85 &#x000b5;m</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 308.406px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Normal</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 262.594px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>75-85 &#x000b5;m</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 308.406px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early thinning</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 262.594px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>60-75 &#x000b5;m</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 308.406px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate damage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 262.594px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>&#x0003c;60 &#x000b5;m</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 308.406px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Advanced damage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: RNFL, retinal nerve fiber layer.</p>
        <p>Thinning of the macular ganglion cell complex often precedes field loss.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Comprehensive Clinical Staging Model</bold>
</p>
        <p>A combined practical staging approach:</p>
        <p>History of Trauma&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Angle Assessment (Gonioscopy)&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;IOP Measurement&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;OCT + Visual Field&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Stage Determination&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0; &#x02193;Target IOP Setting&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prognostic Staging Factors</bold>
</p>
        <p>Poor prognosis is associated with the following risk factors:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Extensive angle recession (&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed presentation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intraocular foreign body&#x02013;related siderosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric age</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advanced optic nerve cupping at diagnosis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric Staging Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Children may develop the following complications:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Faster optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>More aggressive scarring</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher surgical failure rates</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Staging must include monitoring of axial length and amblyopia risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Application of Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>Staging determines the following management parameters:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Target IOP</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Medication intensity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Timing of surgery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Follow-up frequency</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual prognosis counseling&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r150">[150]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table24" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;25.&#x000a0;Follow-Up Based on Stage</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Follow-Up Frequency</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Suspect</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>6-12 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Mild</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Every 4-6 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Every 3-4 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Severe</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Every 1-3 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Post-surgical</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Frequent early monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Key Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession glaucoma may develop years after trauma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Structural staging often precedes functional loss.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Staging must be dynamic; progression reclassifies the stage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trauma history must be revisited even decades later.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Although traumatic glaucoma lacks a unique universal staging system, a combination of mechanistic, structural, and functional criteria provides a comprehensive framework for classification. Staging guides therapeutic aggressiveness, target IOP setting, and long-term surveillance planning. Lifelong monitoring is critical, especially in patients with extensive angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r152">[152]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s16" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis varies depending on the underlying cause of traumatic glaucoma. Conditions such as traumatic hyphema, iritis, steroid response, lens-related glaucoma, ghost cell glaucoma, hemolytic glaucoma, and hemosiderotic glaucoma tend to have a better prognosis, provided the underlying cause of elevated IOP is addressed.</p>
        <p>The prognosis of traumatic glaucoma is highly variable and depends on the mechanism of injury, the extent of angle damage, the timing of diagnosis, the severity of optic nerve involvement, and the adequacy of IOP control. While some patients experience transient IOP elevation with complete recovery, others develop chronic progressive optic neuropathy that may result in permanent visual impairment or blindness. Early recognition and appropriate management can significantly improve visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Prognosis Based on the Mechanism of Injury</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hyphema-associated glaucoma:&#x000a0;Acute IOP elevation due to hyphema often has a favorable prognosis if treated promptly. Most cases resolve with medical therapy and careful monitoring. However, higher-grade hyphema (Grade III-IV), rebleeding, and prolonged IOP elevation increase the risk of optic nerve damage and corneal blood staining. Patients with sickle cell disease or trait have a worse prognosis due to an increased risk of optic neuropathy at lower IOP levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Angle recession glaucoma:&#x000a0;Angle recession carries a lifelong risk of glaucoma development. When more than 180&#x000b0; of the angle is involved, the probability of chronic glaucoma increases substantially. Prognosis depends on the extent of structural trabecular damage and the response to therapy. Many cases remain stable for years before IOP elevation occurs, emphasizing the need for long-term surveillance. Once glaucomatous optic neuropathy develops, progression resembles primary open-angle glaucoma but may be more resistant to medical therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r119">[119]</xref></p>
        <p>Lens-induced or pupillary block mechanisms:&#x000a0;When IOP elevation results from lens subluxation or phacolytic processes, timely surgical correction often leads to good outcomes. Delay in management may result in irreversible optic nerve injury.</p>
        <p>Intraocular foreign bodies&#x02013;related glaucoma:&#x000a0;The prognosis worsens significantly if metallic intraocular foreign bodies cause siderosis or chalcosis. Early removal improves outcomes, but chronic toxicity may lead to progressive retinal degeneration and refractory glaucoma.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Prognosis Based on the Severity of Optic Nerve Damage</bold>
</p>
        <p>Visual prognosis correlates strongly with the stage of disease at diagnosis.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early stage: If treated promptly, visual function is often preserved.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate stage: Controlled IOP may stabilize vision, but some visual field loss is permanent.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advancedstage: Even with aggressive IOP lowering, further progression may occur.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>End-stage: Vision may be limited to light perception or no light perception.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r153">[153]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Structural optic nerve damage is irreversible; therefore, prevention of progression is the primary therapeutic goal.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Surgical Prognosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical outcomes in traumatic glaucoma vary:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Trabeculectomy:&#x000a0;This procedure has higher failure rates due to posttraumatic scarring and inflammation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Glaucoma drainage devices: These devices often demonstrate better long-term success in secondary glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cyclodestructive procedures: These procedures are&#x000a0;reserved for refractory cases; they may preserve comfort but not vision.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Repeated trauma or multiple surgeries can worsen the prognosis due to cumulative tissue damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r154">[154]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Paediatric Prognosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Children may experience:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Faster optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>More aggressive inflammatory response</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher amblyopia risk</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Long-term visual prognosis depends not only on IOP control but also on the timely management of amblyopia and refractive correction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r155">[155]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table25" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;26. Factors Associated With Poor Prognosis</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Risk Factor</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Impact on Prognosis</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Extensive angle recession (&#x0003e;180&#x000b0;)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Increased chronic glaucoma risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed presentation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Greater optic nerve damage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Recurrent hyphema</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Optic nerve compromise</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elevated IOP &#x0003e;40 mmHg for a prolonged period</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Accelerated neuropathy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOFB-related siderosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive retinal toxicity</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Severe optic disc cupping at diagnosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Limited visual recovery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 316.474px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Poor medication adherence</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 255.526px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Disease progression</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold> Long-Term Outlook</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is a lifelong condition in many cases. Even patients initially classified as glaucoma suspects require annual follow-up because glaucoma may manifest years after the original injury. Long-term monitoring includes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP measurement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>OCT</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual field testing</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Stability over several years suggests a good prognosis, but the risk of late progression remains.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r156">[156]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual Outcome Expectations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients treated early often maintain useful vision.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate damage may allow preservation of functional central vision.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advanced disease may result in permanent loss of peripheral vision.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>End-stage cases may require low vision rehabilitation.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Psychosocial implications, especially in young patients with trauma, should be addressed as part of long-term care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Overall Prognostic Summary</bold>
</p>
        <p>The prognosis of traumatic glaucoma ranges from excellent to guarded, depending on the mechanism, severity, and timeliness of intervention. Acute IOP elevation without optic nerve damage is associated with a favorable outcome. Chronic angle recession glaucoma requires lifelong surveillance and may necessitate surgical management. Once optic neuropathy develops, the damage is irreversible; therefore, early detection and aggressive IOP control remain the most important determinants of long-term visual preservation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r157">[157]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s17" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Resistant IOP, despite multiple medical and surgical management, can rarely occur with certain etiologies within traumatic glaucoma. Ultimately, this may lead to severe, irreversible glaucomatous changes to the optic disc, leading to irreversible blindness if the IOP cannot be controlled.</p>
