<p>Hypotension and fatigue</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 255.729px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroids</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 317.271px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hyperglycemia (systemic)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Major Vision-Threatening Complications</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive glaucomatous optic neuropathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal blood staining</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endophthalmitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Phthisis bulbi</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table36" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table&#x000a0;37. Summary of Complication Risk by Stage</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 599px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 25.0674px;">
                <td style="height: 25.0674px; width: 250.625px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Disease Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 25.0674px; width: 322.375px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Major Risk</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250.625px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute phase</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 322.375px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Corneal edema and optic neuropathy</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250.625px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Subacute phase</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 322.375px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>PAS formation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250.625px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Chronic phase</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 322.375px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250.625px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Post-surgical&#x000a0;phase</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 322.375px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hypotony and infection</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: PAS, peripheral anterior synechiae.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession &#x0003e;180&#x000b0; significantly increases the risk of glaucoma throughout life.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyphema rebleed is most common within 5 days.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical scarring increases failure rates in trauma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early IOP control prevents irreversible optic nerve damage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong follow-up is mandatory.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s18" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Postoperative care in traumatic glaucoma is critical because outcomes are heavily influenced by inflammation control, wound healing modulation, IOP stabilization, and prevention of secondary complications. Due to the altered ocular anatomy and increased scarring response following trauma, these patients require closer monitoring than typical primary glaucoma cases.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Immediate Postoperative Care (First 1-2 Weeks)</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>IOP monitoring:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Day 1, week 1, and frequent early follow-up</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Watch for:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypotony (&#x0003c;5 mmHg)</p></list-item><list-item><p>IOP spikes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tube occlusion (if drainage device used)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Inflammation control:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Intensive topical corticosteroids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gradual taper over weeks</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cycloplegics for ciliary body stabilization</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Traumatic eyes often exhibit exaggerated inflammatory responses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r126">[126]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Wound assessment:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Check for:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Bleb leak (Seidel test)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Conjunctival integrity</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tube position</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal endothelial status<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r27">[27]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Prevention of early complications:&#x000a0;</bold>Early recognition and prompt management of postoperative complications are essential to prevent permanent visual loss and ensure optimal surgical outcomes.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table37" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 38. Early Postoperative Complications in Traumatic Glaucoma and Their Management</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Complication</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Early Signs</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Management</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Hypotony</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Shallow AC</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>AC reformation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Choroidal effusion</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Peripheral vision change</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Steroids&#x000a0;with or without drainage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Sudden pain</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Emergency intervention</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Tube occlusion</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elevated IOP</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical revision</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: AC, anterior chamber; IOP, intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intermediate Postoperative Phase (1-3 Months) </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Scar modulation:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Digital massage (trabeculectomy)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suture lysis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>5-fluorouracil injections (if needed)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Trauma patients have a higher risk of bleb fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r158">[158]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Medication adjustment:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Gradual reduction of steroids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Restart IOP-lowering therapy if necessary</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Visual rehabilitation assessment:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Refraction correction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Address induced astigmatism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor for cataract progression&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r159">[159]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Postoperative Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is often a lifelong condition, and patients require ongoing ophthalmic follow-up to monitor IOP and detect disease progression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Monitoring schedule:</bold>
</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table38" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 39.&#x000a0;Recommended Follow-Up Schedule for Long-Term Monitoring in Traumatic Glaucoma</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Stage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Follow-Up Frequency</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early stable</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Every 3-4 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Moderate damage</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Every 3 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Advanced disease</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Every 1-2 months</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Post-surgical unstable</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>As needed</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Long-term assessments:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP measurement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gonioscopy annually</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>OCT retinal nerve fiber layer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual field testing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal endothelial evaluation (tube cases)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r160">[160]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Rehabilitation Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma often affects young, working-age patients. Rehabilitation extends beyond IOP control.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual rehabilitation:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Refractive correction
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Spectacles</p></list-item><list-item><p>Contact lenses (if the cornea is stable)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Low vision aids for advanced disease
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Magnifiers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Telescopic lenses</p></list-item><list-item><p>Electronic magnification devices</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Occupational rehabilitation
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Workplace modifications</p></list-item><list-item><p>Visual ergonomics counseling&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r161">[161]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric rehabilitation:</bold>
</p>
        <p>Children require:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Amblyopia therapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual development monitoring</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Educational support</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Early visual rehabilitation is critical to prevent long-term developmental delays and optimize visual outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Psychological support:</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic injury may lead to:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Posttraumatic stress disorder</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Depression</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anxiety</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Referral to mental health professionals should be considered as part of holistic care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r162">[162]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Special Postoperative Scenarios </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Tube shunt patients:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor corneal endothelium</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Watch for tube exposure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assess for diplopia</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Trabeculectomy patients:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong risk of bleb-related infection</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Educate patients on the signs of endophthalmitis:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Redness</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pain</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vision drop&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Cyclodestructive procedures:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor for hypotony</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Watch for phthisis bulbi</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Patient Education (Critical Component)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients must understand:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong risk of&#x000a0;glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Importance of medication adherence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Need for regular follow-up</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptoms of acute IOP elevation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Signs of bleb infection</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Noncompliance with medications or follow-up is a major cause of disease progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r163">[163]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prognostic Counseling</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients should be informed that:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic nerve damage is irreversible</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The goal is disease stabilization</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgery controls IOP but does not restore vision</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trauma increases the risk of progression&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r164">[164]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Multidisciplinary Rehabilitation Approach</bold>
</p>
        <p>Optimal recovery in patients with traumatic glaucoma often requires a multidisciplinary approach that addresses visual function, psychological well-being, and functional independence.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table39" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 40. Multidisciplinary Team Members and Their Roles in the Rehabilitation of Patients With Traumatic Glaucoma</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Team Member</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Role</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ophthalmologist</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>IOP control and surgery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Optometrist</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Visual rehabilitation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Low vision specialist</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Adaptive devices</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Psychologist</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trauma support</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Occupational therapist</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Functional adaptation</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviation: IOP,&#x000a0;intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic eyes tend to scar aggressively.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The early postoperative period is critical.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts are often preferred in trauma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong follow-up is essential.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rehabilitation is as important as IOP control.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Postoperative care in traumatic glaucoma requires meticulous inflammation control, careful IOP monitoring, wound healing modulation, and proactive management of complications. Because traumatic glaucoma often affects younger individuals and may progress unpredictably, long-term follow-up and comprehensive rehabilitation&#x02014;including visual, occupational, and psychological support&#x02014;are essential for optimizing functional outcomes and quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s19" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Management of traumatic glaucoma frequently requires a multidisciplinary approach because the condition often coexists with complex ocular injuries and systemic trauma. Early and appropriate consultations improve diagnostic accuracy, optimize treatment outcomes, and reduce long-term complications.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ophthalmology Subspecialty Consultations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Glaucoma specialist:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Persistent elevated IOP despite medical therapy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Angle recession &#x0003e;180&#x000b0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Progressive optic nerve damage</p></list-item><list-item><p>Need for surgical intervention</p></list-item><list-item><p>Refractory glaucoma</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Role
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Advanced gonioscopic evaluation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Target IOP determination</p></list-item><list-item><p>Laser planning</p></list-item><list-item><p>Surgical decision-making (trabeculectomy vs tube shunt)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r165">[165]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Retina specialist:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Vitreous hemorrhage</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal detachment</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intraocular foreign body</p></list-item><list-item><p>Traumatic maculopathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Siderosis/chalcosis suspicion</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Role
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>B-scan ultrasonography</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pars plana vitrectomy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intraocular foreign body removal</p></list-item><list-item><p>ERG monitoring</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Retinal pathology may significantly affect visual prognosis independent of glaucoma severity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r166">[166]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cornea specialist:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Corneal blood staining</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal edema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tube-cornea touch</p></list-item><list-item><p>Endothelial decompensation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Traumatic corneal laceration</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Role
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Anterior segment reconstruction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Keratoplasty planning</p></list-item><list-item><p>Endothelial cell monitoring&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r167">[167]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Oculoplastic surgeon:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Orbital fractures</p></list-item><list-item><p>Eyelid lacerations</p></list-item><list-item><p>Globe rupture</p></list-item><list-item><p>Canalicular injury&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r168">[168]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic Consultations </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Emergency medicine/trauma team:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Polytrauma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Head injury</p></list-item><list-item><p>Blunt or penetrating ocular trauma</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Ensures stabilization before ophthalmic intervention.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Internal medicine/cardiology:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Systemic contraindications to beta-blockers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Comorbid hypertension</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anticoagulation therapy management&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r169">[169]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Hematology:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Sickle cell disease or trait (hyphema risk)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bleeding disorders</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anticoagulated patients</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Sickle cell patients are at higher risk for optic nerve damage at lower IOP levels.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric consultation (if child):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Amblyopia risk</p></list-item><list-item><p>Developmental monitoring</p></list-item><list-item><p>Medication dosing adjustments<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r170">[170]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Neurology/neurosurgery:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Associated head trauma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Traumatic optic neuropathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Orbital apex syndrome</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Infectious disease:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Intraocular foreign body&#x02013;related endophthalmitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Orbital cellulitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Post-surgical infection</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Rehabilitation and Support Consultations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Low vision specialist:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Advanced glaucomatous damage</p></list-item><list-item><p>Central visual field involvement</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Interventions
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Optical aids</p></list-item><list-item><p>Electronic magnifiers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Adaptive visual devices&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r171">[171]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Occupational therapy:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Functional impairment</p></list-item><list-item><p>Return-to-work planning</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mobility adaptation</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Psychological/psychiatric support:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Indications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assault-related trauma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Posttraumatic stress disorder</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anxiety</p></list-item><list-item><p>Depression related to vision loss</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma frequently affects young adults, making psychosocial care essential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r148">[148]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table40" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 41.&#x000a0;Multidisciplinary Care Summary Table</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
