<p>Functional recovery</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Psychology</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trauma-related distress</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Mental health support</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations:&#x000a0;IOFB, intraocular foreign body; IOP,&#x000a0;intraocular pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Indications for Urgent Referral</bold>
</p>
        <p>Immediate referral is required in cases of:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP &#x0003e;40-50 mmHg</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Total hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suspected globe rupture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sudden visual loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe optic nerve edema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r170">[170]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Key Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession glaucoma may present years after trauma; therefore, long-term follow-up is essential.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sickle cell disease significantly alters the management of hyphema.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts often require the involvement of a glaucoma subspecialist.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multidisciplinary care improves both visual and functional outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychological counseling is often overlooked but crucial.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma frequently requires coordinated care among ophthalmic subspecialists and systemic healthcare providers. Because trauma can involve multiple ocular structures and systemic systems, early consultation enhances diagnostic accuracy, optimizes treatment strategies, and improves long-term visual and functional outcomes. A multidisciplinary approach is particularly important in pediatric patients, polytrauma cases, and those requiring surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r170">[170]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s20" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients should be given relevant information and educated&#x02014;using layman's terms&#x02014;about the causes of their conditions, treatment options, and a forward management plan. When surgical intervention is recommended, the risks, benefits, and expected outcomes should be explained so that patients can make informed decisions regarding their care.</p>
        <p>Deterrence and patient education are essential components in the management of traumatic glaucoma. Because this condition often affects young, working-age individuals and may develop years after the initial injury, prevention of ocular trauma and long-term awareness of glaucoma risk are critical for reducing visual morbidity. Effective patient counseling improves adherence, enhances early detection, and minimizes disease progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Primary Prevention (Trauma Deterrence)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Preventing ocular trauma is the most effective strategy for reducing the incidence of traumatic glaucoma.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Protective eyewear:&#x000a0;</bold>Patients should be advised to use certified protective eyewear in the following environments:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Industrial workplaces</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Construction environments</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Laboratories</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Agricultural settings</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contact sports</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Racquet sports</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Firearms use</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Polycarbonate lenses provide high impact resistance and are strongly recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r172">[172]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Sports safety education:&#x000a0;</bold>Appropriate eye protection should be encouraged in sports activities.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of face shields in hockey</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Eye guards in squash and racquetball</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Protective goggles in basketball</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Youth athletes are at particular high risk and require early education.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Road safety:&#x000a0;</bold>Preventive measures include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Helmet use among motorcyclists</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Seatbelt adherence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid driving under the influence</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Blunt ocular trauma from motor vehicle accidents is a major contributor globally.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r173">[173]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Violence prevention and public awareness:&#x000a0;</bold>Community-based awareness programs may reduce assault-related eye injuries, especially in high-risk regions.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Secondary Prevention (After Ocular Trauma)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with ocular trauma&#x02014;even without initial IOP elevation&#x02014;should be counseled regarding the risk of delayed-onset glaucoma.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key education points:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Glaucoma may develop years after trauma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Regular follow-up is mandatory</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession &#x0003e;180&#x000b0; increases lifetime risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP monitoring should continue indefinitely&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r172">[172]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Medication Adherence Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Poor medication adherence is a major cause of progression. Patients should be instructed on the following medication practices:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Correct instillation technique</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Punctal occlusion to reduce systemic absorption</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Timing and frequency of drops</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Importance of not skipping doses</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Demonstration during clinic visits improves compliance.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Education on Warning Signs</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients&#x000a0;should be educated about symptoms requiring urgent evaluation, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Sudden vision decrease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Eye pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Halos around lights</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe headache</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Redness with vision change</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sudden increase in floaters</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Early recognition of these symptoms can prevent irreversible optic nerve damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r174">[174]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Post-Surgical Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients undergoing trabeculectomy or tube surgery should be informed about the following postoperative risks and precautions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Risk of infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptoms of bleb leak</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Importance of avoiding eye rubbing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Need for lifelong monitoring</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Signs of endophthalmitis&#x02014;pain, redness, and vision loss&#x02014;require immediate attention.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parents and caregivers should receive counseling regarding the following aspects of care:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Importance of follow-up</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Compliance with amblyopia therapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Protective eyewear for sports</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring academic performance</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Children may not reliably report visual symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r175">[175]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Lifestyle and Systemic Health Counseling</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although traumatic glaucoma is injury-related, systemic optimization improves outcomes through the following measures:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Control of diabetes and hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoidance of smoking</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cautious use of steroids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Regular exercise</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Psychosocial Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic events may cause emotional distress. Patients should be informed about the following support services:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Availability of psychological support</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vision rehabilitation services</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occupational therapy, if needed</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Addressing psychosocial impact improves quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r120">[120]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Education for Special Populations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Patients with sickle cell disease:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased risk of optic damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Seek care immediately with hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid dehydration</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> High-risk occupations:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Annual ophthalmic screening</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Employer safety compliance&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r176">[176]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table41" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 42. Patient Education Summary Table</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 798px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Education Area</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Key Message</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Goal</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Trauma Prevention</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Use protective eyewear</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Reduce injury risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Delayed Risk</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Glaucoma may occur years later</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lifelong monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35.7084px;">
                <td style="height: 35.7084px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Medication adherence</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.7084px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Do not skip drops</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.7084px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prevent progression</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Warning signs</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Seek urgent care if symptoms occur</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Avoid optic damage</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Post-surgical care</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Monitor for infection</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prevent complications</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pediatric care</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ensure compliance</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prevent amblyopia</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 226.632px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Psychosocial support</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 282.368px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Address trauma impact</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 250px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Improve quality of life</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Key Counseling Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic glaucoma can be silent initially.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic nerve damage is irreversible.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early detection preserves vision.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Protective eyewear dramatically reduces risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong surveillance is essential in angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Summary</bold>
</p>
        <p>Deterrence and patient education are foundational to reducing the burden of traumatic glaucoma. Preventive strategies&#x02014;particularly the use of protective eyewear&#x02014;significantly reduce the incidence of ocular trauma. For patients with ocular injury, education on the risk of delayed-onset glaucoma, strict adherence to therapy, recognition of warning signs, and lifelong follow-up are crucial for preserving vision. Comprehensive patient counseling, combined with multidisciplinary rehabilitation support, enhances long-term functional outcomes and quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r172">[172]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s21" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma presents unique diagnostic and management challenges due to its variable onset, heterogeneous mechanisms, and potential for delayed progression. Awareness of key clinical pearls, common pitfalls, and preventive strategies is essential to optimize patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical Pearls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Glaucoma may develop years after trauma. A normal IOP immediately after injury does not exclude future risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession involving more than&#x000a0;180&#x000b0; significantly increases the lifetime risk of glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic glaucoma is often unilateral, and asymmetry should prompt careful gonioscopic comparison with the fellow eye.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP spikes in&#x000a0;patients with sickle cell disease require urgent management, even at relatively modest elevations.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic nerve damage is irreversible; early intervention is critical.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts often outperform trabeculectomy in traumatic glaucoma due to increased scarring risk.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostaglandin analogues should be avoided during active inflammation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Steroid-induced IOP elevation is common and must be monitored carefully.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Structural OCT changes may precede visual field defects.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lifelong follow-up is mandatory in cases of angle recession.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Diagnostic Pitfalls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to perform gonioscopy in patients with trauma&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assuming hyphema-related IOP elevation is transient without follow-up</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overlooking angle recession in remote trauma history</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Missing Intraocular foreign body in subtle injuries</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Misdiagnosing optic neuropathy as traumatic optic nerve injury instead of glaucomatous damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed referral to a glaucoma specialist&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Therapeutic Pitfalls</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Excessive steroid use leading to steroid-induced glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Premature discontinuation of medications once IOP normalizes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delayed surgical referral in progressive optic neuropathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inadequate inflammation control before surgery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to counsel patients regarding the lifelong risk of glaucoma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r126">[126]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Disposition Considerations </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Urgent referral required if:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>IOP &#x0003e;40-50 mmHg</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Total hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sudden vision loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suspected globe rupture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suprachoroidal hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive optic disc cupping&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r177">[177]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Outpatient management appropriate if:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Mild IOP elevation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>No optic nerve damage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reliable follow-up assured</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Monitoring Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with&#x000a0;angle recession require annual lifelong gonioscopy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Structural monitoring (OCT retinal nerve fiber layer) should be routine</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual field progression may be asymmetric</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The fellow eye should be carefully&#x000a0;evaluated <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Prevention Strategies </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Primary prevention:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Protective eyewear in sports and high-risk occupations</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Helmet and seatbelt use</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Workplace safety compliance</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Secondary prevention:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early IOP monitoring after trauma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Education regarding delayed glaucoma risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prompt treatment of hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Tertiary prevention:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Aggressive IOP lowering in established disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rehabilitation services for advanced cases</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychological support when needed&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Special Clinical Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric cases require amblyopia management</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Polytrauma patients may require multidisciplinary care</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sickle cell trait significantly alters the management of hyphema</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trauma-induced glaucoma can coexist with other ocular pathologies,&#x000a0;including retinal detachment, cataract, and corneal injury.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>High-Yield Board Examination Points</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession is characterized by widening of the ciliary body band.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angle recession involving more than 180&#x000b0; increases the risk of glaucoma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ghost cell glaucoma occurs weeks after a vitreous hemorrhage.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tube shunts are preferred in secondary glaucomas.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sickle cell disease increases the risk of optic neuropathy at lower IOP levels.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is unpredictable and may present long after the inciting injury. The most critical pearls include performing gonioscopy in all trauma cases, recognizing the lifelong risk of angle recession glaucoma, monitoring for steroid response, and ensuring sustained IOP control. Avoiding diagnostic and therapeutic pitfalls&#x02014;particularly inadequate follow-up&#x02014;can significantly improve visual outcomes. The most effective long-term strategy includes prevention through protective eyewear and patient education.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s22" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>All healthcare professionals should ensure thorough patient assessment. Interprofessional communication should be clear to ensure patients are treated promptly, especially in trauma situations. All clinicians, not just ophthalmologists&#x000a0;but also&#x000a0;family practitioners and optometrists who have a role in front-line healthcare as the initial point of medical contact after a traumatic event or within eye emergency departments, should have a good understanding of the disease, its pathophysiology, investigations required, and treatment options to allow smooth discussion and handoff between teams.</p>
        <p>Traumatic glaucoma is a complex secondary glaucoma requiring coordinated interprofessional care to optimize visual outcomes and patient safety. Because the condition often follows multisystem trauma and may progress unpredictably over time, effective collaboration among healthcare professionals is essential to ensure timely diagnosis, appropriate intervention, long-term monitoring, and rehabilitation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r127">[127]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Interprofessional Roles and Responsibilities</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold> Ophthalmologist (primary care lead):</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Perform a comprehensive ocular assessment, including gonioscopy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Identify the mechanism of IOP elevation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Establish a target IOP</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Initiate appropriate medical or surgical management</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Coordinate subspecialty referrals&#x000a0;when necessary</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Educate the patient regarding the need for lifelong follow-up</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The ophthalmologist serves as the central coordinator of patient care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r126">[126]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Glaucoma specialist:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluate progressive or refractory cases</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Plan advanced surgical intervention</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optimize long-term disease control</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Guide postoperative care and complication management</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Advanced practice providers:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Assist in monitoring IOP and visual function</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reinforce medication adherence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Provide patient education</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Identify early complications requiring escalation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Nurses:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Provide medication administration education</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Demonstrate proper eye drop technique</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor postoperative recovery</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Screen for medication-related adverse effects</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reinforce follow-up compliance</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Active nursing involvement significantly improves treatment adherence and patient safety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold> Pharmacists:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Review medication interactions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Counsel patients on systemic risks of beta-blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor contraindications, such as asthma and cardiac disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ensure appropriate dosing and patient understanding</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Pharmacist engagement reduces medication-related adverse events.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Retina and cornea specialists:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Manage associated posterior segment injury</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Address corneal complications</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluate Intraocular foreign body&#x02013;related toxicity&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Primary care physicians/internists:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Manage systemic comorbidities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluate the cardiovascular safety of glaucoma medications</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Coordinate care in patients with sickle cell disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Low vision and rehabilitation specialists:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Provide adaptive visual aids</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assist occupational reintegration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Improve quality of life&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r5">[5]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Mental health professionals:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Address trauma-related psychological distress</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Support coping mechanisms</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Improve treatment adherence and engagement&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r77">[77]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Table 43. </bold>Core Skills Required for Team-Based Care</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table42" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Skill Area</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Application in Traumatic Glaucoma</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Communication</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clear explanation of lifelong risk</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clinical vigilance</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early detection of delayed glaucoma</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical planning</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Multidisciplinary input</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Medication management</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Prevent adverse effects</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Patient counseling</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Reinforce adherence</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Risk stratification</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Identify high-risk patients</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Strategy for Care Coordination</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Early multidisciplinary evaluation:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with trauma should receive coordinated ophthalmic assessment.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Immediate referral pathways must be clearly defined.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r165">[165]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Structured follow-up protocol:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Develop standardized monitoring intervals.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use EMR reminders to prevent missed follow-up.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Postoperative surveillance:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Team-based wound assessment.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early identification of hypotony or infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r78">[78]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Long-term monitoring strategy:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Annual gonioscopy for angle recession.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Periodic OCT and visual fields.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Ethical Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Informed consent regarding lifelong risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Clear communication of surgical risks</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid overtreatment in early stages</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Equitable access to protective eyewear and rehabilitation services</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Address the occupational implications of vision loss</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Patient autonomy and shared decision-making are essential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Patient-Centered Care Approach</bold>
</p>
        <p>Effective interprofessional collaboration improves:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Medication adherence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early complication detection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual function preservation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychological well-being</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Return-to-work success</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Patients should actively participate in decision-making and self-monitoring.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Improving Patient Safety</bold>
</p>
        <p>Key safety strategies include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring for steroid-induced IOP rise</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Screening for systemic contraindications</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early recognition of hyphema rebleed</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prompt management of elevated IOP</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prevention of bleb infection</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Standardized care pathways reduce adverse events.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r178">[178]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Communication Best Practices</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Use the teach-back method to confirm understanding.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Provide written instructions post-surgery.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ensure handoffs between emergency and ophthalmology teams are structured.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Document the extent of angle recession clearly for long-term tracking.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Quality Improvement Initiatives</bold>
</p>
        <p>Healthcare systems can improve outcomes by:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Implementing trauma screening protocols</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Promoting protective eyewear campaigns</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tracking surgical outcomes in secondary glaucoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Developing interdisciplinary case conferences&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r179">[179]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold> Table 44.&#x000a0;</bold>Interprofessional Care Model Summary</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-133078.table43" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Phase of Care</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Primary Team Members</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Focus</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute trauma</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Emergency + ophthalmology</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Stabilization</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Early glaucoma risk</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ophthalmology + nursing</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Monitoring</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Progressive disease</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Glaucoma specialist</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Target IOP</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical phase</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Surgical team + nursing</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Complication prevention</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Long-term</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ophthalmology + PCP</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lifelong surveillance</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Advanced vision loss</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rehab + psychology</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Quality of life&#x000a0;</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations: IOP, intraocular pressure; PCP,&#x000a0;phase of care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Takeaways</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic glaucoma requires lifelong monitoring.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Early interprofessional involvement improves visual outcomes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Medication safety requires pharmacist collaboration.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical outcomes improve with coordinated postoperative care.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychological and occupational rehabilitation are critical components of holistic care.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Enhancing healthcare team outcomes in traumatic glaucoma requires coordinated, patient-centered, interprofessional collaboration. Clinicians, advanced practitioners, nurses, pharmacists, rehabilitation specialists, and mental health professionals each play vital roles in optimizing visual outcomes, ensuring medication safety, preventing complications, and supporting long-term quality of life. Structured communication, shared decision-making, and clearly defined care pathways significantly improve patient safety and overall team performance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133078.r152">[152]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133078.s23">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
